#strong[\2. Batch Size and Gradient Accumulation:]

#strong[Relationship to Learning Rate:]

Large batch training requires learning rate scaling:

$ eta_(b a t c h = B) = eta_(b a t c h = B_0) dot.op sqrt(B / B_0) $

This comes from noise scale theory: larger batches have less gradient
noise, so can use larger steps.

#strong[Memory-Constrained Batch Size:]

If GPU memory limits batch size to $B_(m a x)$, use gradient
accumulation:

$ B_(e f f e c t i v e) = B_(m a x) times N_(a c c u m u l a t i o n) $

#strong[Example:]

- GPU memory allows: $B_(m a x) = 4$

- Desired batch size: $B_(d e s i r e d) = 32$

- Gradient accumulation steps:
  $N_(a c c u m u l a t i o n) = 32 / 4 = 8$

