  - #emph[Rule:] Use LR finder (Smith, 2015) and pick 1/10 of steepest
    descent point

  - #emph[Compute Impact:] No direct impact on memory/speed, but wrong
    LR wastes epochs

- #strong[Batch Size:] 16-32 for 24GB GPU, 4-8 with gradient
  accumulation for 12GB

  - #emph[Why:] Larger batches = more stable gradients but higher memory

  - #emph[Memory Formula:] GPU memory $approx$ (params $times$ 4 bytes
    $times$ batch size $times$ 1.3 overhead)

  - #emph[Gradient Accumulation:] Effective batch = per\_device\_batch
    $times$ accumulation\_steps

  - #emph[Trade-off:] Batch 32 converges in 50% fewer steps than batch
    8, but uses 4x memory

- #strong[Epochs:] 3-5 for large datasets ($>$10K), 10-20 for small
  ($<$1K)

  - #emph[Why:] Small datasets need more exposure; large datasets risk
    overfitting

  - #emph[Early Stopping:] Stop if validation loss increases for 3
    consecutive evaluations

  - #emph[Overfitting Indicator:] Train-val loss gap $>$ 0.5 indicates
    severe overfitting

- #strong[Weight Decay:] 0.01 (standard), 0.1 for small datasets, 0.001
  for domain adaptation

  - #emph[Why:] L2 regularization prevents large weight updates

  - #emph[Formula:] Effective update =
    $- eta \( nabla L + lambda_(W D) theta \)$

  - #emph[When to Increase:] High overfitting (train-val gap $>$ 0.5)

- #strong[Warmup Steps:] 100-500 steps (or 10% of total steps)

  - #emph[Why:] Prevents initial training instability from cold start
    with high LR

  - #emph[Schedule:] Linear warmup from 0 to max LR, then cosine decay
    to 0

#strong[Quick Start Configurations:]

- #strong[Small Dataset ($<$10K):] LR=5e-5, Batch=8, Epochs=10, WD=0.1,
  Warmup=10%

- #strong[Large Dataset ($>$100K):] LR=1e-5, Batch=32, Epochs=2,
  WD=0.001, Warmup=5%

- #strong[Domain Adaptation:] LR=3e-6, Batch=16, Epochs=3, WD=0.01,
  Warmup=15%

- #strong[Task-Specific:] LR=1e-4, Batch=16, Epochs=5, WD=0.01,
  Warmup=10%

#strong[Memory Requirements (GPT-2 124M on 24GB GPU):]

- Batch 4: ~4GB GPU memory (safe for any 8GB+ card)

- Batch 16: ~12GB GPU memory (needs 16GB+ card)

- Batch 32: ~20GB GPU memory (needs 24GB+ A100/RTX 4090)

- #emph[Pro Tip:] Use gradient accumulation to simulate larger batches:
  batch=4 $times$ accum=8 = effective 32

#strong[Success Indicators:]

- Training loss should decrease within first 500 steps; if not, LR is
  wrong

- Validation loss should track training loss closely (gap $<$ 0.3) for
  healthy training

- Gradient norms should be 0.1-10.0; if $>$100, gradients exploding
  (reduce LR or add clipping)

- Perplexity should drop from ~50 (untrained) to 5-15 (well-tuned) on
  target domain

]
=== Advanced Fine-Tuning Techniques
<advanced-fine-tuning-techniques>
#strong[\1. Mixout Regularization:]

Stochastically mix pre-trained and current weights:

$ tilde(theta)_i = cases(delim: "{", theta_i^(P T) & upright("with probability ") p, theta_i & upright("with probability ") 1 - p) $

Typically $p = 0.9$. Prevents catastrophic forgetting while allowing
adaptation.

#strong[\2. Child-Tuning:]

Identify and update only task-relevant parameters:

$ theta_i^(t + 1) = cases(delim: "{", theta_i^t - eta nabla_(theta_i) cal(L) & upright("if ") \| nabla_(theta_i) cal(L) \| > tau, theta_i^t & upright("otherwise")) $

where $tau$ is threshold. Reduces effective parameters by 50-70%.

#strong[\3. Lottery Ticket Hypothesis for Fine-Tuning:]

Find sparse subnetwork at initialization:

$ m_i = bb(I) \[ \| theta_i^(P T) \| > upright("percentile") \( \| theta^(P T) \| \, p \) \] $

Fine-tune only masked parameters:
$theta_i^(t + 1) = theta_i^t - eta m_i nabla_(theta_i) cal(L)$

