== Extended Theory: Pre-Training Deep Dive
<extended-theory-pre-training-deep-dive>
=== Scaling Laws for Language Models
<scaling-laws-for-language-models>
Pre-training performance follows predictable scaling laws discovered by
Kaplan et al. (2020):

#strong[Power Law Relationships:]

$ cal(L) \( N \) & = (N_c / N)^(alpha_N) quad upright("(Model size scaling)")\
cal(L) \( D \) & = (D_c / D)^(alpha_D) quad upright("(Data size scaling)")\
cal(L) \( C \) & = (C_c / C)^(alpha_C) quad upright("(Compute scaling)") $

where:

- $N$: Number of parameters

- $D$: Dataset size (tokens)

- $C$: Compute budget (PetaFLOP-days)

- $alpha_N approx 0.076$, $alpha_D approx 0.095$, $alpha_C approx 0.050$

#strong[Chinchilla Scaling Laws (Hoffmann et al., 2022):]

For compute-optimal training:

$ N_(o p t) prop C^0.50 \, quad D_(o p t) prop C^0.50 $

#strong[Implication:] Double compute $arrow.r.double$ increase both
parameters AND data by $sqrt(2) approx 1.4 times$.

#strong[Example - Chinchilla vs Gopher:]

- #strong[Gopher:] 280B params, 300B tokens ($N gt.double D$)

- #strong[Chinchilla:] 70B params, 1.4T tokens ($N approx D$)

- #strong[Result:] Chinchilla outperforms despite 4x fewer parameters!

