    parameter count, showing diminishing returns but continuous
    improvement; (2) #emph[Data Size Scaling] - loss follows
    $cal(L) \( D \) prop D^(- 0.095)$ with more training tokens,
    emphasizing the importance of large corpora; (3) #emph[Chinchilla vs
    Gopher Strategy] - comparing parameter-heavy (Gopher: 280B params,
    300B tokens) vs balanced approach (Chinchilla: 70B params, 1.4T
    tokens), where Chinchilla achieves superior performance despite 4×
    fewer parameters by following compute-optimal scaling; (4)
    #emph[Compute-Optimal Scaling] - showing $N_(o p t) prop C^0.5$ and
    $D_(o p t) prop C^0.5$, meaning doubling compute budget requires
    increasing both model size and data by $sqrt(2) approx 1.4 times$.
    These laws (Kaplan et al. 2020, Hoffmann et al. 2022) are
    fundamental for efficient resource allocation in modern LLM
    training.
