== Understanding Pre-Training: From First Principles
<understanding-pre-training-from-first-principles>
=== The Intuition Behind Next-Token Prediction
<the-intuition-behind-next-token-prediction>
Pre-training a language model might seem deceptively simple: predict the
next word. But why does this work so well? Let's build intuition from
the ground up.

#strong[The Core Insight:] To predict the next token accurately, a model
must learn:

+ #strong[Syntax:] Grammar rules (e.g., \"The cat\" is likely followed
  by a verb, not another noun)

+ #strong[Semantics:] Word meanings and relationships (e.g., \"fire\" is
  hot, \"ice\" is cold)

+ #strong[World Knowledge:] Facts about the world (e.g., \"The capital
  of France is\" $arrow.r$ \"Paris\")

+ #strong[Reasoning:] Logical connections (e.g., \"If it's raining, I
  should bring\" $arrow.r$ \"an umbrella\")

+ #strong[Context:] Long-range dependencies (e.g., pronouns referring to
  entities mentioned paragraphs earlier)

Think of it like this: imagine you're reading a mystery novel with the
last page torn out. To guess what happens, you need to understand
character motivations, plot threads, genre conventions, and world
physics. The model learns all of this just by repeatedly trying to
\"fill in the blank.\"

#strong[Why This Works - The Compression Perspective:]

From information theory, the best compression algorithm for data is one
that perfectly models the data distribution. Language models are
essentially compression algorithms: they learn to represent text
efficiently by discovering patterns. To compress well, they must
understand deeply.

Mathematically, we can derive this connection. The cross-entropy loss
used in training is:

$ cal(L) = - 1 / T sum_(t = 1)^T log P_theta \( x_t \| x_(< t) \) $

This is exactly the #strong[bits per token] (or nats per token if using
natural log). A model with loss 2.0 nats/token would need
$e^2.0 approx 7.4$ bits per token to encode the text - about 0.93 bytes
per token. The better the model predicts, the better it compresses.

Let's derive why minimizing cross-entropy learns the true distribution:

#strong[Derivation: Cross-Entropy and KL Divergence]

The cross-entropy between true distribution $P$ and model distribution
$Q$ is:

$ H \( P \, Q \) & = - sum_x P \( x \) log Q \( x \)\
 & = - sum_x P \( x \) log P \( x \) + sum_x P \( x \) log frac(P \( x \), Q \( x \))\
 & = H \( P \) + D_(K L) \( P parallel Q \) $

where $H \( P \)$ is the entropy of the true distribution (constant) and
$D_(K L)$ is the KL divergence. Since
$D_(K L) \( P parallel Q \) gt.eq 0$ with equality iff $P = Q$,
minimizing cross-entropy is equivalent to minimizing KL divergence,
which is minimized when $Q = P$.

Therefore, perfect next-token prediction implies learning the true
language distribution!

