== Extended Theory: LoRA Mathematical Deep Dive
<extended-theory-lora-mathematical-deep-dive>
=== Eckart-Young-Mirsky Theorem: Optimal Low-Rank Approximation
<eckart-young-mirsky-theorem-optimal-low-rank-approximation>
#strong[The Big Picture - Why Low-Rank Approximation Works:]

Before diving into the proof, understand the fundamental insight:
#emph[Not all dimensions in a weight update matrix $Delta W$ are equally
important];. Most of the \"signal\" concentrates in a few dominant
directions, while the rest is noise. The Eckart-Young theorem makes this
precise and provides the optimal way to capture the signal while
discarding noise.

#strong[Theorem Statement (Eckart-Young-Mirsky, 1936):]

Let $Delta W in bb(R)^(d times k)$ be any matrix with Singular Value
Decomposition (SVD):

$ Delta W = U Sigma V^T = sum_(i = 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i u_i v_i^T $

where:

- $U = \[ u_1 \, dots.h \, u_d \] in bb(R)^(d times d)$: Left singular
  vectors (orthonormal)

- $V = \[ v_1 \, dots.h \, v_k \] in bb(R)^(k times k)$: Right singular
  vectors (orthonormal)

- $Sigma = upright("diag") \( sigma_1 \, dots.h \, sigma_(min \( d \, k \)) \)$:
  Singular values with
  $sigma_1 gt.eq sigma_2 gt.eq dots.h.c gt.eq sigma_r gt.eq 0$

Then the best rank-$r$ approximation to $Delta W$ in both Frobenius norm
and spectral norm is:

$ Delta W_r = sum_(i = 1)^r sigma_i u_i v_i^T = U_r Sigma_r V_r^T $

where $U_r in bb(R)^(d times r)$, $Sigma_r in bb(R)^(r times r)$,
$V_r in bb(R)^(r times k)$ are truncated matrices.

#strong[Optimality Guarantee:]

For any other rank-$r$ matrix $tilde(W)_r$:

$ parallel Delta W - Delta W_r parallel_F & lt.eq parallel Delta W - tilde(W)_r parallel_F quad upright("(Frobenius norm)")\
parallel Delta W - Delta W_r parallel_2 & lt.eq parallel Delta W - tilde(W)_r parallel_2 quad upright("(Spectral norm)") $

Moreover, the approximation error is exactly:

$ parallel Delta W - Delta W_r parallel_F & = sqrt(sum_(i = r + 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i^2) = sqrt(sigma_(r + 1)^2 + sigma_(r + 2)^2 + dots.h.c)\
parallel Delta W - Delta W_r parallel_2 & = sigma_(r + 1) quad upright("(largest discarded singular value)") $

#strong[Proof Sketch for Frobenius Norm:]

#emph[Step 1: Decompose error]

$ parallel Delta W - tilde(W)_r parallel_F^2 & = parallel sum_(i = 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i u_i v_i^T - tilde(W)_r parallel_F^2\
 & = parallel sum_(i = 1)^r sigma_i u_i v_i^T + sum_(i = r + 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i u_i v_i^T - tilde(W)_r parallel_F^2 $

#emph[Step 2: Use orthogonality]

Since ${ u_i }$ and ${ v_i }$ are orthonormal bases:

$ parallel Delta W - tilde(W)_r parallel_F^2 = sum_(i = 1)^r sigma_i^2 parallel u_i v_i^T - upright("proj")_(tilde(W)_r) \( u_i v_i^T \) parallel_F^2 + sum_(i = r + 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i^2 $

#emph[Step 3: Apply projection inequality]

For any rank-$r$ matrix $tilde(W)_r$, the projection satisfies:

$ parallel u_i v_i^T - upright("proj")_(tilde(W)_r) \( u_i v_i^T \) parallel_F^2 gt.eq 0 $

with equality only when
$upright("proj")_(tilde(W)_r) \( u_i v_i^T \) = u_i v_i^T$ for
$i lt.eq r$.

#emph[Step 4: Conclude optimality]

The minimum error is achieved when we keep the first $r$ terms exactly:

$ #box(stroke: black, inset: 3pt, [$ min_(upright("rank") \( tilde(W)_r \) = r) parallel Delta W - tilde(W)_r parallel_F^2 = sum_(i = r + 1)^(min \( d \, k \)) sigma_i^2 $]) $

This is attained uniquely by
$Delta W_r = sum_(i = 1)^r sigma_i u_i v_i^T$.

#strong[Connection to LoRA:]

LoRA parameterizes the rank-$r$ approximation as $Delta W approx B A$
where:

$ B & = U_r Sigma_r^(1 \/ 2) in bb(R)^(d times r)\
A & = Sigma_r^(1 \/ 2) V_r^T in bb(R)^(r times k) $

This gives $B A = U_r Sigma_r V_r^T = Delta W_r$, which is the
Eckart-Young optimal approximation!

#strong[Why This Matters for LoRA:]

The theorem guarantees that if the \"true\" fine-tuning update
$Delta W_(t r u e)$ has rapidly decaying singular values (common in
practice), then LoRA's rank-$r$ parameterization $B A$ can approximate
it very well:

$ upright("Approximation Error") = sqrt(sigma_(r + 1)^2 + sigma_(r + 2)^2 + dots.h.c + sigma_(min \( d \, k \))^2) $

If singular values decay exponentially ($sigma_i prop e^(- lambda i)$),
then error decreases exponentially with $r$.

#strong[SVD and Low-Rank Approximation]

#figure([],
  caption: [
    #strong[Eckart-Young Low-Rank Approximation via SVD.] The full
    update matrix $Delta W$ is decomposed into $U Sigma V^T$ where
    singular values $sigma_i$ are ordered by magnitude. Keeping only the
    top $r$ singular values and their corresponding singular vectors
    (shown in darker shading) gives the optimal rank-$r$ approximation.
    LoRA parameterizes this as $Delta W_r = B A$ where $B$ captures the
    left structure and $A$ captures the right structure. The
    approximation error is exactly the Frobenius norm of discarded
    singular values.
  ]
)
