=== LoRA vs Full Fine-Tuning: Theoretical Comparison
<lora-vs-full-fine-tuning-theoretical-comparison>
#strong[Expressiveness:]

Full FT can access full $bb(R)^(d times k)$ space ($d k$ dimensions).

LoRA restricted to rank-$r$ manifold ($r \( d + k \)$ dimensions, where
$r lt.double min \( d \, k \)$).

#strong[Sample Complexity:]

$ N_(f u l l) & tilde.op O (frac(d k, epsilon.alt^2))\
N_(L o R A) & tilde.op O (frac(r \( d + k \), epsilon.alt^2)) $

For $r lt.double min \( d \, k \)$: LoRA requires exponentially fewer
samples to reach same accuracy!

#strong[Generalization Bound:]

Rademacher complexity for LoRA:

$ cal(R)_n \( L o R A \) lt.eq frac(C sqrt(r \( d + k \)), sqrt(n)) $

vs Full FT:

$ cal(R)_n \( F u l l \) lt.eq frac(C sqrt(d k), sqrt(n)) $

#strong[Implication:] LoRA has better generalization with limited data!

=== Advanced LoRA Variants
<advanced-lora-variants>
#strong[\1. AdaLoRA (Adaptive LoRA):]

Dynamically adjusts rank per layer:

$ r_l = arg max_r {bb(E) \[ cal(L) \] - lambda sum_l r_l} $

Prunes singular values below threshold:

$ tilde(Sigma) = upright("diag") \( max \( sigma_i - tau \, 0 \) \) $

#strong[\2. LoRA+:]

Adds low-rank to both forward and residual:

$ h = \( W_0 + Delta W_(L o R A) \) x + Delta W_(r e s i d u a l) x $

#strong[\3. Compacter:]

Uses Kronecker product for higher compression:

$ Delta W = \( A_1 times.circle A_2 \) \( B_1 times.circle B_2 \)^T $

Parameters: $O \( r sqrt(d) + r sqrt(k) \)$ instead of
$O \( r \( d + k \) \)$.

