=== Quantization-Aware Training vs Post-Training Quantization
<quantization-aware-training-vs-post-training-quantization>
#strong[Post-Training Quantization (PTQ) - Used in QLoRA:]

$ W_(q u a n t) = Q \( W_(t r a i n e d) \) $

Advantages:

- No retraining needed

- Fast deployment

- Works for existing models

#strong[Quantization-Aware Training (QAT):]

$ min_W cal(L) \( Q \( W \) \) $

Advantages:

- Better accuracy

- Model adapts to quantization

#strong[QLoRA's Hybrid Approach:]

+ Base model: PTQ to NF4 (frozen)

+ LoRA adapters: Full precision FP16 (trainable)

Best of both worlds!

=== Mixed-Precision Training Mathematics
<mixed-precision-training-mathematics>
#strong[Forward Pass:]

$ h_(F P 16) = sigma \( upright("Cast")_(F P 16) \( W_(N F 4) \) dot.op x_(F P 16) + B_(F P 16) A_(F P 16)^T dot.op x_(F P 16) \) $

#strong[Backward Pass:]

$ frac(partial cal(L), partial A) & = frac(partial cal(L), partial h) dot.op B dot.op x^T quad upright("(FP16)")\
frac(partial cal(L), partial B) & = frac(partial cal(L), partial h) dot.op x dot.op A^T quad upright("(FP16)") $

#strong[Key Insight:] Gradients never flow through quantized weights,
only through FP16 adapters!

=== Theoretical Accuracy Bounds
<theoretical-accuracy-bounds>
#strong[Approximation Error:]

Let $f_(F P 16)$ be full-precision model, $f_(Q L o R A)$ be quantized
model:

$ \| f_(F P 16) \( x \) - f_(Q L o R A) \( x \) \| lt.eq L parallel W_(F P 16) - W_(N F 4) parallel parallel x parallel $

where $L$ is Lipschitz constant of activation function.

#strong[Empirical Findings (Dettmers et al., 2023):]

- NF4 + LoRA: 99.5% of BF16 + LoRA performance

- Int4 + LoRA: 97.2% of BF16 + LoRA performance

- Int8 + LoRA: 99.8% of BF16 + LoRA performance

=== Memory-Compute Trade-off Analysis
<memory-compute-trade-off-analysis>
#strong[Compute Cost:]

QLoRA adds dequantization overhead:

$ T_(Q L o R A) = T_(d e q u a n t) + T_(c o m p u t e) + T_(L o R A) $

where:

- $T_(d e q u a n t) approx 0.1 times T_(c o m p u t e)$ (10% overhead)

- $T_(L o R A) approx 0.05 times T_(c o m p u t e)$ (5% overhead)

#strong[Total:] 15% slower but 75% less memory!

#strong[Pareto Frontier:]

$ upright("Efficiency") \( b \, r \) = frac(upright("Accuracy") \( b \, r \), upright("Memory") \( b \, r \) times upright("Time") \( b \, r \)) $

Optimal point: $b = 4$ bits, $r = 64$ for 7B models.

=== Practical Considerations
<practical-considerations>
#strong[\1. Batch Size Selection:]

QLoRA allows larger batch sizes:

$ b_(m a x) = frac(M_(G P U) - M_(m o d e l), M_(a c t i v a t i o n)) $

#strong[Example (24GB GPU, 7B model):]

- FP16: $b_(m a x) = 1$ (28 GB needed)

- QLoRA: $b_(m a x) = 8$ (8 GB model + 16 GB activations)

#strong[\2. Convergence Speed:]

Larger batches $arrow.r.double$ fewer steps but same wall-clock time:

$ T_(t o t a l) = N_(s a m p l e s) / b times t_(s t e p) $

QLoRA: Same total time, better statistics!

