=== Unified PEFT Framework Theory
<unified-peft-framework-theory>
#strong[General Formulation:]

All PEFT methods can be expressed as:

$ W_(e f f e c t i v e) = W_0 + Delta W \( Theta \) $

where $Theta$ is small set of trainable parameters and
$Delta W : bb(R)^(\| Theta \|) arrow.r bb(R)^(d times k)$ is
method-specific function.

#strong[Method-Specific Instantiations:]

$ upright("LoRA:") quad & Delta W \( Theta \) = B A^T \, quad Theta = { A \, B }\
upright("Adapter:") quad & Delta W \( Theta \) = upright("adapter module") \, quad Theta = { W_(d o w n) \, W_(u p) }\
upright("Prefix:") quad & Delta W \( Theta \) = f \( P_K \, P_V \) \, quad Theta = { P_K \, P_V }\
upright("Prompt:") quad & Delta W \( Theta \) = \[ P ; dot.op \] \, quad Theta = { P } $

=== Theoretical Comparison: Sample Complexity
<theoretical-comparison-sample-complexity>
#strong[PAC Learning Bound:]

For $epsilon.alt$-accurate model with confidence $1 - delta$, sample
complexity:

$ N gt.eq 1 / epsilon.alt^2 (d_(upright("eff")) log d_(upright("eff")) / epsilon.alt + log 1 / delta) $

where $d_(upright("eff"))$ is effective dimensionality.

#strong[Effective Dimensions:]

$ d_(upright("full")) & = d k\
d_(upright("LoRA")) & = r \( d + k \)\
d_(upright("adapter")) & = 2 r d times L\
d_(upright("prompt")) & = l d $

#strong[Implication:] PEFT methods require exponentially fewer samples!

#strong[Empirical Sample Complexity:]

