== Extended Theory: LangGraph Mathematical Foundations
<extended-theory-langgraph-mathematical-foundations>
=== Graph Theory Foundations
<graph-theory-foundations>
#block[
#strong[Definition 7] (State Graph). #emph[A LangGraph state graph is a
directed multigraph $G = \( V \, E \, S \, delta \, lambda \)$ where:]

- #emph[$V$ is a finite set of nodes (computation units)]

- #emph[$E subset.eq V times V$ is a set of edges (transitions)]

- #emph[$S$ is the state space]

- #emph[$delta : V times S arrow.r S$ is the node transformation
  function]

- #emph[$lambda : V times S arrow.r V union { tack.t }$ is the routing
  function ($tack.t$ means END)]

]
#strong[Execution Semantics:]

Starting from initial state $s_0$ and entry node $v_0$:

#block[
$s arrow.l s_0$, $v arrow.l v_0$, $t arrow.l 0$
$s arrow.l delta \( v \, s \)$ $v arrow.l lambda \( v \, s \)$
$t arrow.l t + 1$ $s$

]
#strong[Graph Properties:]

+ #strong[Reachability:] Node $v'$ is reachable from $v$ if there exists
  path $v arrow.r.squiggly v'$

+ #strong[Cyclicity:] Graph is cyclic if
  $exists v : v arrow.r.squiggly v$

+ #strong[Termination:] Graph terminates if all paths from $v_0$ reach
  $tack.t$ in finite steps

#strong[Path Analysis:]

Set of all possible paths from $v_0$:

$ cal(P) = { \( v_0 \, v_1 \, dots.h \, v_n \) : v_i in V \, \( v_i \, v_(i + 1) \) in E \, v_n = tack.t } $

Path probability under state distribution:

$ P \( upright(bold(v)) \| s_0 \) = product_(i = 0)^(n - 1) P \( v_(i + 1) \| v_i \, s_i \) $

where $s_i$ is state after executing $v_i$.

#strong[Expected Execution Length:]

$ bb(E) \[ T \] = sum_(upright(bold(v)) in cal(P)) P \( upright(bold(v)) \) dot.op \| upright(bold(v)) \| $

For cyclic graphs with bounded loops:

$ bb(E) \[ T \| upright("cycle") \] = frac(1, 1 - p_(upright("loop"))) $

where $p_(upright("loop"))$ is probability of looping.

=== State Management Theory
<state-management-theory>
#strong[State Space:]

$ S = S_1 times S_2 times dots.h times S_n $

where $S_i$ is the type of state variable $i$.

#strong[State Update Function:]

Node $v$ produces update $Delta s$:

$ s_(t + 1) = s_t xor Delta s $

where $xor$ is the merge operator (e.g., dict update, list append).

#strong[Reducer Functions:]

For state variable with reducer $r$:

$ s_i^(t + 1) = r \( s_i^t \, Delta s_i \) $

Common reducers:

$ r_(upright("replace")) \( s \, Delta s \) & = Delta s\
r_(upright("append")) \( s \, Delta s \) & = s thin \| \| thin Delta s\
r_(upright("add")) \( s \, Delta s \) & = s + Delta s\
r_(upright("max")) \( s \, Delta s \) & = max \( s \, Delta s \) $

#strong[State Size Analysis:]

For append-only state:

$ \| s_t \| = \| s_0 \| + sum_(i = 1)^t \| Delta s_i \| $

grows linearly with steps.

#strong[Memory Complexity:]

Total memory:

$ M \( t \) = \| s_t \| + sum_(v in V) \| C_v \| $

where $C_v$ is code/cache for node $v$.

#strong[State Compression:]

Apply compression function $phi.alt : S arrow.r S'$ where
$\| S' \| < \| S \|$:

$ s'_t = phi.alt \( s_t \) $

with information loss:

$ upright("Loss") = H \( S \) - I \( S ; phi.alt \( S \) \) $

where $H$ is entropy, $I$ is mutual information.

=== Conditional Branching Theory
<conditional-branching-theory>
#strong[Branching Function:]

$ lambda : V times S arrow.r V union { tack.t } $

can be decomposed into predicates:

$ lambda \( v \, s \) = cases(delim: "{", v_1 & upright("if ") p_1 \( s \), v_2 & upright("if ") p_2 \( s \), dots.v, v_k & upright("if ") p_k \( s \), tack.t & upright("otherwise")) $

#strong[Decision Tree Representation:]

Branching forms a decision tree with depth $d$:

$ upright("Complexity") = O \( 2^d \) $

for binary decisions.

#strong[Entropy of Branching:]

Uncertainty in next node:

$ H \( V_(upright("next")) \| s \) = - sum_(v in V) P \( v \| s \) log P \( v \| s \) $

Low entropy → predictable flow, High entropy → complex routing.

#strong[Conditional Mutual Information:]

Information state $s$ provides about next node:

$ I \( V_(upright("next")) ; s \) = H \( V_(upright("next")) \) - H \( V_(upright("next")) \| s \) $

#strong[Optimal Branching:]

Minimize expected cost:

$ lambda^(*) = "arg min"_lambda bb(E)_(s tilde.op cal(D)) \[ C \( lambda \( v \, s \) \) \] $

where $C$ is cost function (e.g., execution time, error rate).

=== Checkpoint and Persistence Theory
<checkpoint-and-persistence-theory>
#strong[Checkpoint Definition:]

A checkpoint is a tuple:

$ upright("CP")_t = \( s_t \, v_t \, h_t \, upright("meta")_t \) $

where:

- $s_t$ is state at time $t$

- $v_t$ is current node

- $h_t$ is execution history

- $upright("meta")_t$ is metadata (timestamp, thread ID, etc.)

#strong[Checkpoint Storage:]

Space for $T$ checkpoints:

$ upright("Storage") = sum_(t = 1)^T \| s_t \| + \| h_t \| + O \( 1 \) $

