#block[
#strong[Critical PPO hyperparameters explained:]

- #strong[KL coefficient ($beta$): 0.01-0.05]

  - #emph[Purpose:] Prevents model from drifting too far from SFT
    baseline

  - #emph[Too low ($<$0.005):] Model exploits reward model, generates
    nonsense

  - #emph[Too high ($>$0.1):] Model barely improves, stays too close to
    SFT

  - #emph[Adaptive tuning:] Use TRL's `adap_kl_ctrl=True` to auto-adjust

- #strong[Clip range ($epsilon.alt$): 0.1-0.2]

  - #emph[Purpose:] Limits how much policy can change in one update

  - #emph[Typical value:] 0.2 for most LLM tasks

  - #emph[Smaller ($tilde.op$0.1):] More conservative updates, slower
    but safer

- #strong[GAE lambda ($lambda$): 0.95]

  - #emph[Purpose:] Balances bias-variance tradeoff in advantage
    estimation

  - #emph[High ($>$0.95):] Lower variance, higher bias

  - #emph[Low ($<$0.9):] Higher variance, can be unstable

- #strong[PPO epochs ($K$): 4-10]

  - #emph[Purpose:] Number of gradient updates per batch of rollouts

  - #emph[Too few ($<$3):] Inefficient use of rollout data

  - #emph[Too many ($>$10):] Risk of overfitting to current batch

- #strong[Batch size: 64-256 prompts]

  - #emph[Rollout batch:] Number of responses generated per iteration

  - #emph[Minibatch:] Subset used for each gradient step (typically
    16-64)

  - #emph[Larger batches:] More stable but slower; higher memory

#strong[Learning rate schedule for RL phase:]

- #strong[Policy LR:] $1 times 10^(- 6)$ to $5 times 10^(- 6)$ (much
  lower than SFT!)

- #strong[Value LR:] $1 times 10^(- 5)$ (can be higher since value
  function trains from scratch)

- #strong[Warmup:] Not typically used in RL phase

- #strong[Cosine decay:] Often beneficial for long RL training

#strong[Compute requirements (7B model):]

- #strong[Memory:] $tilde.op$40-60 GB GPU RAM (policy + reference +
  value + reward models)

- #strong[Speed:] $tilde.op$2-5 minutes per PPO iteration (64 prompts, 4
  epochs)

- #strong[Total time:] 10K-50K iterations typical ($tilde.op$1-7 days on
  8x A100)

]
#block[
#strong[Pitfall 1: Reward hacking / Model collapse]

- #emph[Symptoms:]

  - Reward keeps increasing but generated text is gibberish

  - Model generates repetitive tokens or exploits specific patterns

  - KL divergence explodes ($>$10-20)

- #emph[Solutions:]

  - Increase KL coefficient $beta$ (try doubling: $0.02 arrow.r 0.04$)

  - Use KL penalty adaptive control (`adap_kl_ctrl=True`)

  - Add length penalty:
    $r_(upright("total")) = r_(upright("RM")) - alpha dot.op \| y \|$

  - Inspect generated samples every 100 iterations

#strong[Pitfall 2: Value function divergence]

- #emph[Symptoms:]

  - Value loss keeps increasing instead of decreasing

  - Advantages have extreme values ($\| A_t \| > 100$)

  - Policy loss becomes unstable

- #emph[Solutions:]

  - Clip value function predictions:
    $V_(upright("clipped")) = V_(upright("old")) + upright("clip") \( V - V_(upright("old")) \, - epsilon.alt_v \, epsilon.alt_v \)$

  - Normalize advantages: $hat(A) = \( A - mu_A \) \/ sigma_A$ per batch

  - Reduce value function learning rate

  - Use gradient clipping: `max_grad_norm=1.0`

#strong[Pitfall 3: Slow or no improvement]

- #emph[Symptoms:]

  - Mean reward plateaus or decreases

  - KL stays very low ($<$0.01)

  - Generated responses identical to SFT model

- #emph[Diagnosis:]

  - KL coefficient $beta$ too high → reduce by half

  - Learning rate too low → try $5 times 10^(- 6)$

  - Reward model not discriminative → check RM validation accuracy

  - PPO epochs too few → increase to 6-8

#strong[Pitfall 4: Out of memory (OOM) errors]

- #emph[Cause:] Loading 4 models simultaneously (policy, reference,
  value, reward)

- #emph[Solutions:]

  - Reduce batch size (try 32 or 16 prompts)

  - Use gradient checkpointing: `model.gradient_checkpointing_enable()`

  - Offload reference model to CPU (since it's frozen)

  - Use 8-bit quantization for reward model: `load_in_8bit=True`

  - Reduce max generation length

#strong[Debugging checklist for PPO:]

+ Monitor key metrics every iteration:

  - Mean reward (should increase)

  - Mean KL (should be $<$5-10)

  - Policy loss (should decrease initially)

  - Value loss (should decrease)

  - Entropy (should decrease slowly)

+ Sample and inspect generations every 100 steps

+ Check advantage distribution: should be roughly balanced (not all
  positive/negative)

+ Verify rollout generation: no truncation errors, proper EOS tokens

+ Compare to SFT baseline: wins should increase over training

#strong[When to stop training:]

- Mean reward plateaus for $>$1000 iterations

- Human evaluation shows no further improvement

- KL budget is exhausted (KL $>$ target threshold consistently)

- Typical range: 10K-50K PPO iterations

]
#block[
+ PPO optimizes policy via clipped objective:
  $L = min \( r_t A_t \, upright("clip") \( r_t \, 1 plus.minus epsilon.alt \) A_t \)$

+ Key components: Policy network, value network, reference model (frozen
  SFT), reward model

+ Advantage estimation uses GAE:
  $hat(A)_t = sum_(l = 0)^oo \( gamma lambda \)^l delta_(t + l)$

+ KL penalty ($beta D_(upright("KL"))$) prevents reward hacking and
  model collapse

+ Critical hyperparameters: $beta in \[ 0.01 \, 0.05 \]$,
  $epsilon.alt in \[ 0.1 \, 0.2 \]$, LR $tilde.op 10^(- 6)$

+ Main challenge: Balancing reward maximization with staying close to
  SFT baseline

+ Success indicators: Increasing reward, stable KL ($<$10), human eval
  improvements

]
