=== Perplexity: The Foundation Metric
<perplexity-the-foundation-metric>
#strong[What is Perplexity? - The Intuitive Understanding]

Imagine you're reading a mystery novel. When a plot twist happens,
you're \"surprised\" - you didn't expect that character to be the
villain. When the detective follows obvious clues, you're not surprised
\- you saw it coming.

Perplexity measures \*\*how surprised a language model is\*\* by the
actual text. Lower surprise = better model. If the model assigns high
probability to the words that actually appear, perplexity is low. If the
model is constantly surprised (\"I didn't expect the next word to be
'the' - I thought it would be 'elephant'!\"), perplexity is high.

#strong[Concrete Example - Let's See It In Action:]

Consider predicting the next word in: \"The cat sat on the \_\_\_\"

#strong[Good Model (Low Perplexity):]

- P(\"mat\") = 0.4

- P(\"floor\") = 0.3

- P(\"chair\") = 0.2

- P(\"ceiling\") = 0.05

- P(\"elephant\") = 0.0001

If the actual word is \"mat\", the model assigned 40% probability -
reasonable guess, low surprise.

#strong[Bad Model (High Perplexity):]

- P(\"elephant\") = 0.3

- P(\"supernova\") = 0.2

- P(\"mat\") = 0.01 ← actual word

- P(\"floor\") = 0.01

The model assigned only 1% probability to the actual word \"mat\" -
terrible guess, high surprise.

#strong[Why Use Perplexity Instead of Raw Probability?]

Three reasons:

+ #strong[Geometric Mean:] Perplexity averages across all tokens. Long
  sequences have tiny probabilities (0.9 × 0.8 × 0.7 × ... → 0.00001),
  but perplexity stays interpretable.

+ #strong[Scale-Invariant:] Probabilities shrink exponentially with
  sequence length. Perplexity remains comparable across short and long
  texts.

+ #strong[Information Theory Connection:] Perplexity =
  $2^(upright("entropy"))$. It represents the \"effective vocabulary
  size\" - how many words the model is genuinely uncertain about at each
  step.

#strong[Interpreting Perplexity Values:]

- #strong[PPL = 1:] Perfect model (assigns probability 1 to correct
  token every time). Impossible in practice except on memorized data.

- #strong[PPL = 10-20:] Excellent model. Means the model is uncertain
  among ~10-20 plausible next words.

- #strong[PPL = 50-100:] Decent model. Still learning patterns but not
  terrible.

- #strong[PPL = 1000+:] Poor model. Essentially random guessing.

- #strong[PPL = Vocab Size:] Random baseline. If vocabulary has 50K
  words, PPL $approx$ 50K means model just picks uniformly.

#strong[Real-World Example:]

GPT-3 (175B parameters) achieves:

- PPL $approx$ 20 on general web text (excellent)

- PPL $approx$ 40 on specialized domains (medical, legal) before
  fine-tuning

- PPL $approx$ 15 on those domains after fine-tuning (improvement!)

The drop from 40 → 15 shows fine-tuning worked - the model is less
surprised by domain-specific vocabulary.

