#block[
#strong[Perplexity Computation:]

- #strong[Sequence Length:] Use max\_length=512-1024 for efficient
  computation \
  #emph[Why:] Longer sequences increase memory usage quadratically in
  attention \
  #emph[Memory:] GPU RAM = batch\_size × seq\_len × hidden\_dim × 4
  bytes × overhead

- #strong[Stride:] Set stride = max\_length / 2 for sliding window
  approach \
  #emph[Why:] Overlapping context prevents truncation artifacts \
  #emph[Trade-off:] Smaller stride = more accurate but slower (2x
  overlap = 2x time)

- #strong[Test Set Size:] Minimum 1000 tokens for reliable perplexity
  estimates \
  #emph[Why:] Variance decreases as $sigma^2 \/ N$ where $N$ is token
  count \
  #emph[Rule:] Standard error \< 0.5 requires ~5000 tokens for
  PPL$approx$20

#strong[Metric Selection Guide:]

- #strong[Perplexity:] Best for language modeling tasks, pre-training
  evaluation

- #strong[BLEU:] Best for machine translation (precision-focused)

- #strong[ROUGE:] Best for summarization (recall-focused)

- #strong[Exact Match + F1:] Best for question answering

- #strong[Human Evaluation:] Gold standard for open-ended generation

#strong[Benchmark Configuration:]

- #strong[Few-Shot Examples:] Use 0-shot baseline, then 1-shot, 5-shot
  for comparison

- #strong[Temperature:] Set to 0 (greedy) for deterministic evaluation

- #strong[Batch Size:] 8-16 for evaluation (larger than training to
  maximize throughput)

]
#block[
#strong[Pitfall 1: Overfitting to Metrics]

- #strong[Symptoms:] BLEU score increases but human evaluation ratings
  decrease

- #strong[Root Cause:] Model learns to game the metric (e.g., copying
  n-grams from input)

- #strong[Diagnosis:] Compare automatic metrics with human ratings on
  held-out set

- #strong[Solution:]

  ```python
  # Always include multiple metrics - don't optimize for just one
  evaluation_suite = {
      'perplexity': compute_perplexity(model, test_data),
      'bleu': compute_bleu_corpus(predictions, references),
      'rouge': compute_rouge_corpus(predictions, references),
      'human_rating': get_human_ratings(predictions)  # Sample 100 examples
  }
  # If metrics diverge, investigate specific examples
  ```

- #strong[Prevention:] Use diverse metric suite, include human
  evaluation regularly

#strong[Pitfall 2: Comparing Across Different Datasets]

- #strong[Symptoms:] Model A has PPL=15 on Dataset X, Model B has PPL=18
  on Dataset Y. Which is better?

- #strong[Root Cause:] Perplexity is dataset-dependent (different
  vocabularies, domains)

- #strong[Solution:] #strong[Never compare perplexity across different
  datasets] \
  Only compare models evaluated on the #emph[same] test set

- #strong[Correct Approach:]

  ```python
  # Evaluate both models on SAME test set
  test_set = load_dataset('common_test_set')
  ppl_a = compute_perplexity(model_a, test_set)  # e.g., 15.2
  ppl_b = compute_perplexity(model_b, test_set)  # e.g., 18.7
  # Now comparison is valid: Model A is better (15.2 < 18.7)
  ```

#strong[Pitfall 3: Ignoring Statistical Significance]

- #strong[Symptoms:] Claim \"Model A beats Model B\" based on 0.002 F1
  difference

- #strong[Root Cause:] Random variance makes small differences
  meaningless

- #strong[Diagnosis:] Run paired t-test (p \< 0.05 required for
  significance)

- #strong[Solution:]

  ```python
  from scipy.stats import ttest_rel

  # Per-example scores (not averages!)
  scores_a = [compute_score(pred_a, ref) for pred_a, ref in zip(preds_a, refs)]
  scores_b = [compute_score(pred_b, ref) for pred_b, ref in zip(preds_b, refs)]

  t_stat, p_value = ttest_rel(scores_a, scores_b)
  mean_diff = np.mean(scores_a) - np.mean(scores_b)

  if p_value < 0.05:
      print(f"Significant difference: {mean_diff:.4f} (p={p_value:.4f})")
  else:
      print(f"No significant difference (p={p_value:.4f})")
  ```

- #strong[Prevention:] Always report confidence intervals and p-values

#strong[Pitfall 4: Not Masking Prompt Tokens in Conditional Generation]

- #strong[Symptoms:] Perplexity seems too good (unrealistically low)

- #strong[Root Cause:] Including prompt tokens in loss calculation
  inflates scores

- #strong[Solution:] Mask prompt with label=-100 (only evaluate on
  generated output)

  ```python
  # Correct: Mask prompt tokens
  labels = input_ids.clone()
  labels[:, :prompt_length] = -100  # Don't compute loss on prompt
  loss = model(input_ids, labels=labels).loss  # Only output tokens
  ```

- #strong[Prevention:] Always use conditional perplexity for
  instruction-following

#strong[Pitfall 5: Using Wrong Tokenizer for Evaluation]

- #strong[Symptoms:] Perplexity dramatically changes after model update

- #strong[Root Cause:] Different tokenizer produces different token
  counts

- #strong[Solution:] Always use the model's native tokenizer, never mix

]
#block[
+ #strong[Perplexity Formula:]
  $upright("PPL") = exp (- 1 / N sum_(i = 1)^N log P \( w_i \| w_(< i) \))$
  is the exponential of average negative log-likelihood. Lower is
  better.

+ #strong[Success Indicators:] \
  \- Perplexity should be \< 20 for good general models, \< 15 after
  fine-tuning \
  \- BLEU \> 0.3 for machine translation, ROUGE-L \> 0.4 for
  summarization \
  \- Exact Match \> 60% and F1 \> 70% for question answering

+ #strong[Metric Limitations:] \
  \- Perplexity: Dataset-dependent, not comparable across different test
  sets \
  \- BLEU: Zero if any 4-gram doesn't match, too harsh for open-ended
  generation \
  \- ROUGE: Doesn't capture semantic meaning, only n-gram overlap \
  \- All automatic metrics: Imperfect proxies for human judgment

+ #strong[Evaluation Hierarchy:] \
  (1) Human evaluation (expensive, gold standard) \
  (2) LLM-as-judge (GPT-4 ratings, cheaper than humans) \
  (3) Automatic metrics (fast, cheap, limited validity)

+ #strong[Statistical Rigor:] \
  \- Always report mean ± std dev from multiple runs (3-5 seeds) \
  \- Use paired t-test for model comparison (p \< 0.05 for significance)
  \
  \- Report 95% confidence intervals with bootstrap (10K samples)

+ #strong[Conditional Perplexity:] For instruction-following, mask
  prompt tokens (label=-100) and compute loss only on generated outputs.
  This prevents artificially low perplexity.

+ #strong[Production Checklist:] \
  (1) Multiple metrics (perplexity + task-specific + human eval) \
  (2) Statistical significance testing (don't trust small differences) \
  (3) Diverse test set (multiple domains, edge cases) \
  (4) Regular monitoring (detect drift in production)

+ #strong[When to Use Which Metric:] \
  \- Pre-training: Perplexity on held-out web text \
  \- Instruction tuning: Conditional perplexity + human preference \
  \- Translation: BLEU + human adequacy ratings \
  \- Summarization: ROUGE + factuality checks \
  \- QA: Exact Match + F1 + human verification

+ #strong[Never Optimize Directly for Evaluation Metrics:] Goodhart's
  Law - \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good
  measure.\" Use metrics for monitoring, not as training objectives.

+ #strong[Benchmark Contamination:] Check if test data was in
  pre-training corpus. Models may have memorized answers. Always
  validate on novel, held-out data.

