#strong[The Fairness Impossibility Theorem:]

#strong[Theorem (Chouldechova, 2017; Kleinberg et al., 2017):]

If the base rates differ between groups (i.e.,
$P \( Y = 1 \| G = g_1 \) eq.not P \( Y = 1 \| G = g_2 \)$), then it is
#emph[impossible] to simultaneously satisfy:

+ Calibration:
  $P \( Y = 1 \| hat(Y) = hat(y) \, G = g \) = P \( Y = 1 \| hat(Y) = hat(y) \)$

+ Equalized odds: $P \( hat(Y) \| Y \, G = g \) = P \( hat(Y) \| Y \)$

+ Demographic parity: $P \( hat(Y) \| G = g \) = P \( hat(Y) \)$

unless the classifier is perfect (TPR = 1, FPR = 0).

#strong[Proof Sketch:]

#strong[Step 1: Assume Different Base Rates]

Let $p_1 = P \( Y = 1 \| G = g_1 \)$ and
$p_2 = P \( Y = 1 \| G = g_2 \)$ with $p_1 eq.not p_2$ (without loss of
generality, $p_1 > p_2$).

#strong[Step 2: Bayes' Rule Decomposition]

For calibration, we need:
$ P \( Y = 1 \| hat(Y) = 1 \, G = g \) = frac(P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 1 \, G = g \) dot.op P \( Y = 1 \| G = g \), P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g \)) $

#strong[Step 3: Apply Equalized Odds Constraint]

If equalized odds holds:
$ P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 1 \, G = g_1 \) & = P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 1 \, G = g_2 \) = T P R\
P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 0 \, G = g_1 \) & = P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 0 \, G = g_2 \) = F P R $

#strong[Step 4: Compute Marginal Positive Rates]

$ P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g_i \) & = P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 1 \, G = g_i \) dot.op P \( Y = 1 \| G = g_i \)\
 & quad + P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| Y = 0 \, G = g_i \) dot.op P \( Y = 0 \| G = g_i \)\
 & = T P R dot.op p_i + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_i \) $

For group 1:
$P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g_1 \) = T P R dot.op p_1 + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_1 \)$

For group 2:
$P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g_2 \) = T P R dot.op p_2 + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_2 \)$

#strong[Step 5: Show Demographic Parity is Violated]

For demographic parity, we need:
$ P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g_1 \) = P \( hat(Y) = 1 \| G = g_2 \) $

Substituting:
$ T P R dot.op p_1 + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_1 \) = T P R dot.op p_2 + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_2 \) $

Simplifying: $ T P R \( p_1 - p_2 \) & = - F P R \( p_1 - p_2 \)\
T P R \( p_1 - p_2 \) & = F P R \( p_2 - p_1 \)\
\( T P R + F P R \) \( p_1 - p_2 \) & = 0 $

Since $p_1 eq.not p_2$ (our assumption), this requires:
$ T P R + F P R = 0 $

But TPR, FPR $in \[ 0 \, 1 \]$, so this is only possible if TPR = FPR =
0 (always predict negative) or by contradiction, requiring TPR = 1 and
FPR = 0 (perfect classifier).

#strong[Step 6: Show Calibration Conflict]

Similarly, enforcing calibration across groups with different base rates
creates tension with equalized odds. The positive predictive values
become:

$ P P V \( g_i \) = frac(T P R dot.op p_i, T P R dot.op p_i + F P R dot.op \( 1 - p_i \)) $

For calibration to hold globally: $ P P V \( g_1 \) = P P V \( g_2 \) $

Expanding and simplifying (similar algebra) shows this conflicts with
equalized odds unless the classifier is perfect.

#strong[Conclusion:] This impossibility result forces practitioners to
make deliberate trade-offs based on application context. There is no
universal \"fair\" classifier when base rates differ.

