=== Embedding Models
<embedding-models>
#strong[Dense Embeddings:]

Transform text to fixed-dimensional vector:

$ upright(bold(e)) = upright("Encoder") \( t e x t \) in bb(R)^d $

#strong[Popular Embedding Models:]

- #strong[Sentence-BERT (SBERT):] Fine-tuned BERT for semantic
  similarity
  $ upright(bold(e)) = upright("mean-pooling") \( upright("BERT") \( t e x t \) \) $

- #strong[OpenAI text-embedding-ada-002:] 1536-dimensional embeddings

- #strong[Instructor-XL:] Task-specific instruction-based embeddings
  $ upright(bold(e)) = upright("Encoder") \( upright("instruction") + t e x t \) $

- #strong[E5/BGE:] State-of-the-art open-source embeddings

#strong[Embedding Quality Metrics:]

- #strong[Cosine Similarity:]
  $ cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = frac(upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)), parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel) = frac(sum_(i = 1)^d u_i v_i, sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d u_i^2) sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d v_i^2)) $

- #strong[Dot Product:] (for normalized embeddings)
  $ upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) = sum_(i = 1)^d u_i v_i $

- #strong[Euclidean Distance:]
  $ d \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) parallel = sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d \( u_i - v_i \)^2) $

- #strong[Mahalanobis Distance:] (accounts for correlation)
  $ d_M \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = sqrt(\( upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) \)^T Sigma^(- 1) \( upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) \)) $
  where $Sigma$ is the covariance matrix.

#strong[Mathematical Foundations of Similarity Metrics - Deep Dive:]

#strong[WHY Similarity Metrics Matter in RAG:]

Retrieval quality directly determines generation quality. If we retrieve
irrelevant documents, the LLM has no chance of producing a correct
answer, no matter how powerful. The choice of similarity metric
determines which documents are considered \"relevant\" - this is the
foundation of the entire RAG system.

#strong[Real-World Impact:]

- #strong[Medical Q&A:] Wrong similarity metric retrieves general health
  advice instead of specific drug interactions → potential patient harm

- #strong[Legal Research:] Missing key precedents because cosine
  similarity focuses on word frequency, not legal concepts

- #strong[Customer Support:] Retrieving syntactically similar but
  semantically different previous tickets

#strong[Step-by-Step Derivation of Cosine Similarity:]

#strong[Intuition:] Cosine similarity measures the #emph[angle] between
two vectors, ignoring magnitude. Two documents about the same topic
should point in the same direction in embedding space, even if one is
much longer.

#strong[Geometric Foundation:]

#strong[Step 1: Recall Dot Product Definition]

The dot product of two vectors measures both angle AND magnitude:
$ upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) = parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel cos theta $

where $theta$ is the angle between vectors.

#strong[Step 2: Isolate the Angle]

To get just the angle (direction), normalize out the magnitudes:
$ cos theta = frac(upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)), parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel) $

This is the cosine of the angle $theta$.

#strong[Step 3: Expand to Component Form]

Express in terms of vector components
$upright(bold(u)) = \( u_1 \, dots.h \, u_d \)$ and
$upright(bold(v)) = \( v_1 \, dots.h \, v_d \)$:

$ upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) & = sum_(i = 1)^d u_i v_i\
parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel & = sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d u_i^2)\
parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel & = sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d v_i^2) $

#strong[Step 4: Combine for Final Formula]

$ cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = frac(sum_(i = 1)^d u_i v_i, sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d u_i^2) sqrt(sum_(i = 1)^d v_i^2)) $

#strong[Properties and Interpretation:]

- #strong[Range:] $cos theta in \[ - 1 \, 1 \]$

  - $cos theta = 1$: Vectors point in exactly same direction (identical
    documents)

  - $cos theta = 0$: Vectors are orthogonal (completely unrelated
    topics)

  - $cos theta = - 1$: Vectors point in opposite directions
    (contradictory content)

- #strong[Scale Invariance:] Duplicating a document doesn't change
  similarity
  $ cos \( alpha upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) quad forall alpha > 0 $

- #strong[Computational Efficiency:] For L2-normalized embeddings
  ($parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel = parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel = 1$):
  $ cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) = sum_(i = 1)^d u_i v_i $
  This is just a dot product (O($d$) instead of O($d$) + normalization)

#strong[Worked Example with Concrete Numbers:]

#strong[Scenario:] Compare two document embeddings in 3D space
(simplified from typical 768D)

#strong[Given:] $ upright(bold(q u e r y)) & = \( 0.5 \, 0.3 \, 0.2 \)\
upright(bold(d o c))_1 & = \( 0.6 \, 0.4 \, 0.1 \) quad upright("(relevant document)")\
upright(bold(d o c))_2 & = \( 0.1 \, 0.1 \, 0.8 \) quad upright("(different topic)") $

#strong[Step 1: Compute Dot Products]

$ upright(bold(q u e r y)) dot.op upright(bold(d o c))_1 & = \( 0.5 \) \( 0.6 \) + \( 0.3 \) \( 0.4 \) + \( 0.2 \) \( 0.1 \)\
 & = 0.30 + 0.12 + 0.02 = 0.44 $

$ upright(bold(q u e r y)) dot.op upright(bold(d o c))_2 & = \( 0.5 \) \( 0.1 \) + \( 0.3 \) \( 0.1 \) + \( 0.2 \) \( 0.8 \)\
 & = 0.05 + 0.03 + 0.16 = 0.24 $

#strong[Step 2: Compute Norms]

$ parallel upright(bold(q u e r y)) parallel & = sqrt(0.5^2 + 0.3^2 + 0.2^2) = sqrt(0.25 + 0.09 + 0.04)\
 & = sqrt(0.38) approx 0.616 $

$ parallel upright(bold(d o c))_1 parallel & = sqrt(0.6^2 + 0.4^2 + 0.1^2) = sqrt(0.36 + 0.16 + 0.01)\
 & = sqrt(0.53) approx 0.728 $

$ parallel upright(bold(d o c))_2 parallel & = sqrt(0.1^2 + 0.1^2 + 0.8^2) = sqrt(0.01 + 0.01 + 0.64)\
 & = sqrt(0.66) approx 0.812 $

#strong[Step 3: Compute Cosine Similarities]

$ cos \( upright(bold(q u e r y)) \, upright(bold(d o c))_1 \) & = frac(0.44, \( 0.616 \) \( 0.728 \)) = 0.44 / 0.448 approx 0.982 $

$ cos \( upright(bold(q u e r y)) \, upright(bold(d o c))_2 \) & = frac(0.24, \( 0.616 \) \( 0.812 \)) = 0.24 / 0.500 approx 0.480 $

#strong[Conclusion:] Document 1 has cosine similarity 0.982 (nearly
parallel, highly relevant), while Document 2 has 0.480 (moderate angle,
less relevant). We would retrieve Document 1.

#strong[Derivation of Euclidean vs Cosine Relationship:]

#strong[Theorem:] For L2-normalized vectors, Euclidean distance and
cosine similarity are related by:

$ d_(upright("Euclidean"))^2 \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = 2 \( 1 - cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) \) $

#strong[Proof:]

#strong[Step 1: Expand Euclidean Distance]

$ d^2 & = parallel upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) parallel^2\
 & = \( upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) \) dot.op \( upright(bold(u)) - upright(bold(v)) \)\
 & = upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(u)) - 2 upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) + upright(bold(v)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) $

#strong[Step 2: Apply Normalization Constraint]

Given
$parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel = parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel = 1$,
we have:
$ upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(u)) & = parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel^2 = 1\
upright(bold(v)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) & = parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel^2 = 1 $

#strong[Step 3: Substitute]

$ d^2 & = 1 - 2 upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) + 1\
 & = 2 - 2 upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v))\
 & = 2 \( 1 - upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) \) $

#strong[Step 4: Recall Cosine Definition]

For normalized vectors:
$upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) = cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \)$

Therefore:
$ d^2 = 2 \( 1 - cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) \) $

#strong[Implications:]

- When embeddings are normalized, cosine similarity and Euclidean
  distance are equivalent (monotonically related)

- Minimizing Euclidean distance $arrow.l.r.double$ Maximizing cosine
  similarity

- This is why most vector databases normalize embeddings before indexing

