+ #strong[Core Mathematical Relationship:] For L2-normalized embeddings:
  $ d_(upright("Euclidean"))^2 \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = 2 \( 1 - cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) \) $
  This means Euclidean distance and cosine similarity are equivalent
  when embeddings are normalized. Always normalize to enable fast cosine
  via dot product.

+ #strong[Metric Choice Hierarchy:]

  - #strong[Default:] Cosine similarity (scale-invariant, handles
    variable document lengths)

  - #strong[Special Case:] Dot product if you explicitly want to favor
    longer documents (rare)

  - #strong[Avoid:] Raw Euclidean distance without normalization (biased
    by magnitude)

+ #strong[Essential Formulas:]
  $ upright("Cosine:") & quad cos \( upright(bold(u)) \, upright(bold(v)) \) = frac(sum_i u_i v_i, sqrt(sum_i u_i^2) sqrt(sum_i v_i^2))\
  upright("Normalized Dot:") & quad upright(bold(u)) dot.op upright(bold(v)) = sum_i u_i v_i quad \( upright("when ") parallel upright(bold(u)) parallel = parallel upright(bold(v)) parallel = 1 \)\
  upright("Recall@k:") & quad frac(\| upright("relevant") sect upright("top-k") \|, \| upright("relevant") \|) $

+ #strong[Success Indicators:]

  - Recall\@5 $gt.eq$ 0.85: Strong retrieval (85% of relevant docs in
    top-5)

  - MRR $gt.eq$ 0.70: Relevant docs consistently in top-2 positions

  - Similarity score std dev $gt.eq$ 0.15: Good separation between
    relevant/irrelevant

  - Embedding norms = 1.0 ± 0.001: Properly normalized

+ #strong[Critical Implementation Details:]

  - ALWAYS L2-normalize embeddings before indexing: ‘emb /
    np.linalg.norm(emb)‘

  - Use asymmetric models (E5, DPR) or prefixes for query vs document
    encoding

  - Monitor embedding model version with metadata (re-index if model
    changes)

  - Set confidence threshold (typically 0.5) to reject
    out-of-distribution queries

  - Batch encoding: Use batch\_size=32-128 for GPU efficiency

+ #strong[Production Monitoring Checklist:]

  - Track median similarity score per hour (alert if drops \>10%)

  - Monitor Recall\@5 on evaluation set weekly (target $gt.eq$ 0.85)

  - Log queries with max\_similarity \< 0.3 (likely OOD or system
    failures)

  - A/B test embedding model changes (new model must beat baseline by
    $gt.eq$ 3% Recall\@5)

  - Automated normalization checks: assert embedding norms $approx$ 1.0

+ #strong[Embedding Dimension Trade-offs:]

  - 384-dim: 2x faster encoding, 70% less storage, -3% recall vs
    1024-dim

  - 768-dim: Balanced (industry standard for most applications)

  - 1024-dim: +2% recall, use only for accuracy-critical domains
    (medical, legal)

  - #emph[Rule:] Start with 768-dim, drop to 384-dim if latency
    critical, upgrade to 1024-dim only if Recall\@5 \< 0.85

+ #strong[The Normalization Imperative:] Un-normalized embeddings in RAG
  systems are a top-3 production bug. Symptoms:

  - Short, highly relevant documents get ranked below long, tangential
    ones

  - Correlation between document length and retrieval score \> 0.3

  - Impossible to set meaningful similarity thresholds (scores scale
    with doc length)

  Fix: One-line normalization before ALL vector operations.

+ #strong[Query-Document Asymmetry:] Generic BERT models encode \"What
  is Python?\" similarly to \"Python is a programming language\" (both
  mention Python). But for RAG, we want query→answer similarity, not
  statement→statement. Use:

  - E5 with prefixes: ‘query: \<text\>‘ vs ‘passage: \<text\>‘

  - DPR: Separate query\_encoder and passage\_encoder

  - Fine-tuned models: Train on (question, answer) pairs from your
    domain

+ #strong[When Embeddings Fail - Know the Limits:]

  - Keyword-heavy queries: \"Find document containing
    PATIENT-ID-123456\" → Use hybrid search (BM25 + embeddings)

  - Multi-hop reasoning: \"What did the person who invented Python also
    create?\" → Embeddings can't handle this, need query decomposition

  - Exact match requirements: Legal citations, code snippets →
    Supplement with exact string matching

