= Quantization-Aware Knowledge Distillation (QAKD)
<quantization-aware-knowledge-distillation-qakd>
== Introduction: The Dual Challenge of Compression
<introduction-the-dual-challenge-of-compression>
=== The Big Picture: Why Combine Quantization and Distillation?
<the-big-picture-why-combine-quantization-and-distillation>
Imagine you're a master chef (large full-precision model) teaching an
apprentice (small student model) who works in a tiny kitchen with
limited tools (quantized weights). The apprentice needs to learn your
techniques #emph[while adapting to their constrained environment];. This
is the essence of Quantization-Aware Knowledge Distillation.

#strong[The Core Problem:]

Modern LLMs face two critical deployment challenges:

+ #strong[Model Size:] GPT-3 (175B params) requires 700GB in FP32, 350GB
  in FP16

+ #strong[Inference Speed:] Autoregressive generation is slow due to
  sequential token generation

#strong[Traditional Solutions (Applied Separately):]

- #strong[Knowledge Distillation (KD):] Train small model mimicking
  large teacher
  $ cal(L)_(upright("KD")) = upright("KL") \( P_(upright("student")) parallel P_(upright("teacher")) \) $
  #emph[Problem:] Student is full-precision (FP32/FP16), still
  memory-intensive

- #strong[Post-Training Quantization (PTQ):] Reduce precision after
  training
  $ W_(upright("quant")) = upright("Round") (W_(upright("FP32")) / Delta) dot.op Delta $
  #emph[Problem:] Accuracy drop (1-3% on INT8, 5-10% on INT4) because
  model wasn't trained for quantization

#strong[The QAKD Innovation:]

Combine both approaches #emph[during training];, allowing the student to
learn from the teacher #emph[while being aware of quantization noise];.

$ cal(L)_(upright("QAKD")) = cal(L)_(upright("KD")) + lambda bb(E) \[ epsilon.alt^2 \] $

where:

- $cal(L)_(upright("KD"))$: Standard distillation loss (learn from
  teacher)

- $bb(E) \[ epsilon.alt^2 \]$: Quantization noise penalty
  ($epsilon.alt = W_q - W$)

- $lambda$: Trade-off hyperparameter (typically $10^(- 4)$ to
  $10^(- 3)$)

#strong[Real-World Motivation:]

Consider deploying a chatbot on mobile devices:

- #strong[Teacher:] LLaMA-2 70B (280GB FP32) - too large for mobile

- #strong[Standard Distillation:] LLaMA-2 7B (28GB FP32) - still too
  large

- #strong[Post-Quantization:] LLaMA-2 7B INT4 (3.5GB) - accuracy drops
  8%

- #strong[QAKD:] LLaMA-2 7B INT4 trained with QAKD (3.5GB) - accuracy
  drop only 2%

#strong[Key Insight:] Training the student #emph[with] quantization
noise present teaches it to be robust to precision loss, rather than
quantizing a model that expects full precision.

=== Historical Context and Evolution
<historical-context-and-evolution>
#strong[Timeline of Compression Techniques:]

+ #strong[2015: Knowledge Distillation (Hinton et al.)]
  $ cal(L)_(upright("KD")) = alpha dot.op upright("CE") \( y \, upright("student") \) + \( 1 - alpha \) dot.op upright("KL") \( upright("teacher") parallel upright("student") \) $
  Demonstrated that \"dark knowledge\" in teacher's soft predictions
  helps student generalize.

+ #strong[2018: Quantization-Aware Training (Jacob et al., Google)]
  Train with fake quantization operators to simulate INT8 inference:
  $ upright("FakeQuant") \( x \) = upright("Quantize") \( x \) arrow.r upright("Dequantize") \( x \) $

+ #strong[2019: TinyBERT, DistilBERT] Applied distillation to BERT,
  achieving 40% size reduction with 97% accuracy retention.

+ #strong[2022: GPTQ, AWQ (Post-Training Quantization)] Advanced PTQ
  methods for LLMs, but still suffer 3-5% accuracy loss on INT4.

+ #strong[2023-Present: QAKD (Unified Approach)] Combines distillation +
  quantization-aware training for optimal compression.

