== Mixed-Precision Training for QAKD
<mixed-precision-training-for-qakd>
=== The Precision Hierarchy in Modern Training
<the-precision-hierarchy-in-modern-training>
#strong[Motivation:]

Not all operations need the same precision. Mixed-precision training
uses different bit-widths for different operations to optimize
speed/accuracy trade-off.

#strong[Typical Precision Hierarchy:]

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 3,
    align: (left,center,left,),
    table.header([#strong[Operation];], [#strong[Precision];], [#strong[Reason];],),
    table.hline(),
    [Forward pass (inference)], [INT8/INT4], [Speed, memory (target for
    deployment)],
    [Forward pass (training)], [FP16], [Balance speed + accuracy],
    [Gradient computation], [FP16], [Sufficient for backprop],
    [Weight updates], [FP32], [Accumulate small gradients without
    underflow],
    [Normalization (LayerNorm)], [FP32], [Prevent numerical
    instability],
    [Loss computation], [FP32], [High precision for stability],
  )]
  , caption: [Mixed-Precision Training Configuration]
  , kind: table
  )

=== Gradient Scaling for Low-Precision Training
<gradient-scaling-for-low-precision-training>
#strong[Problem: Gradient Underflow]

FP16 has limited range: $\[ 6 times 10^(- 8) \, 65504 \]$

Small gradients (e.g., $10^(- 10)$) underflow to zero, stalling
training.

#strong[Solution: Loss Scaling]

+ #strong[Scale loss by large factor] before backprop:
  $ cal(L)_(upright("scaled")) = S dot.op cal(L)_(upright("QAKD")) $
  where $S = 2^10$ to $2^16$ (typically 1024 or 65536)

+ #strong[Gradients are automatically scaled:]
  $ frac(partial cal(L)_(upright("scaled")), partial W) = S dot.op frac(partial cal(L)_(upright("QAKD")), partial W) $

+ #strong[Unscale before optimizer step:]
  $ g_(upright("true")) = g_(upright("scaled")) / S $

+ #strong[Update weights (FP32):]
  $ W^(\( t + 1 \)) = W^(\( t \)) - alpha dot.op g_(upright("true")) $

#strong[Example:]

Without scaling:
$ g = 3 times 10^(- 8) arrow.r^(upright("FP16")) 0 quad upright("(underflow!)") $

With scaling ($S = 1024$):
$ g_(upright("scaled")) = 1024 times 3 times 10^(- 8) = 3 times 10^(- 5) arrow.r^(upright("FP16")) 3 times 10^(- 5) quad upright("(preserved!)") $

=== Fake Quantization for Training
<fake-quantization-for-training>
#strong[Problem:] True INT4/INT8 operations don't support backprop.

#strong[Solution:] Simulate quantization in FP16/FP32, use
straight-through estimator for gradients.

#strong[Forward Pass (Fake Quantization):]

$ W_q = upright("Quantize") \( W \) = Delta dot.op upright("Round") (W / Delta) $

implemented in FP16 (not actual INT4).

#strong[Backward Pass (Straight-Through Estimator):]

Round function has zero gradient everywhere (step function). Use
approximation:

$ frac(partial W_q, partial W) approx 1 quad upright("(identity function)") $

This allows gradients to flow:
$ frac(partial cal(L), partial W) = frac(partial cal(L), partial W_q) dot.op frac(partial W_q, partial W) approx frac(partial cal(L), partial W_q) $

#strong[Justification:]

For small changes $Delta W$, if $W$ doesn't cross a quantization
boundary:
$ upright("Round") \( W + Delta W \) approx upright("Round") \( W \) $

So treating round as identity is reasonable locally.

#figure(image("../visualization/images/qakd/mixed_precision_strategy.png", width: 95.0%),
  caption: [
    #strong[Mixed-Precision Training Strategy:] Left panel shows the
    precision hierarchy used in QAKD training - INT4/INT8 for inference
    simulation, FP16 for forward/backward passes (2-3× speedup), and
    FP32 for weight updates and normalization (numerical stability).
    Right panel demonstrates gradient scaling mechanism (scale factor
    S=1024) that prevents FP16 underflow, keeping gradient magnitudes
    above the $6 times 10^(- 8)$ threshold throughout training.
  ]
)
