#strong[Code Walkthrough - Understanding the Implementation:]

#strong[Why these design choices? - The Deep Reasoning:]

- #strong[Fake Quantization with Straight-Through Estimator:] \
  #emph[Problem:] Round function has zero gradients everywhere except
  discontinuities. \
  #emph[Solution:] Use quantized weights in forward pass, but let
  gradients flow as if no quantization. \
  #emph[Math:]
  $frac(partial W_q, partial W) = frac(partial \( upright("Round") \( W \) \), partial W) approx 1$
  (STE approximation) \
  #emph[Implementation:] `weight + (weight_q - weight).detach()` \
  #emph[Why it works:] Trains model to produce weights that round well,
  not to avoid rounding.

- #strong[Temperature Scaling ($tau = 3$):] \
  #emph[Purpose:] Softens teacher's probability distribution to expose
  \"dark knowledge.\" \
  #emph[Example:] Teacher logits \[8.3, 7.1, 5.2\] $arrow.r$ Probs
  \[0.65, 0.28, 0.07\] (hard) \
  With $tau = 3$: Logits \[2.77, 2.37, 1.73\] $arrow.r$ Probs \[0.45,
  0.32, 0.18\] (soft) \
  #emph[Insight:] Student learns from teacher's \"second-best\" choices,
  not just top prediction. \
  #emph[Multiply by $T^2$:] Compensates for entropy reduction from
  temperature in softmax.

- #strong[Lambda ($lambda = 5 times 10^(- 4)$):] \
  #emph[Intuition:] Balance between teacher imitation and quantization
  friendliness. \
  #emph[Too high ($lambda > 1 times 10^(- 3)$):] Weights snap to grid,
  student ignores teacher. \
  #emph[Too low ($lambda < 1 times 10^(- 5)$):] No quantization
  awareness, accuracy drop at deployment. \
  #emph[Typical:] $cal(L)_(upright("KD")) approx 2.5$,
  $cal(L)_(upright("quant")) approx 0.002$, so
  $lambda dot.op cal(L)_(upright("quant")) approx 0.001$ (tiny but
  cumulative).

- #strong[Mixed-Precision Training (FP16 forward, FP32 updates):] \
  #emph[Speed:] FP16 matrix multiplies are 2-3$times$ faster on modern
  GPUs (Tensor Cores). \
  #emph[Memory:] FP16 activations use half the memory, enabling larger
  batch sizes. \
  #emph[Precision:] Gradients accumulated in FP32 to avoid underflow
  (min FP16 = $6 times 10^(- 8)$). \
  #emph[Loss scaling:] Multiply loss by 1024-65536 before backward to
  prevent gradient underflow. \
  #emph[Critical:] Unscale gradients before optimizer step to maintain
  correct update magnitudes.

- #strong[AWQ Scaling ($alpha = 0.5$):] \
  #emph[Why per-channel:] Different neurons have different activation
  magnitudes. \
  #emph[Formula:] $W'_(i j) = W_(i j) dot.op \( s_i^0.5 \/ s_max^0.5 \)$
  where $s_i = bb(E) \[ \| A_i \( x \) \| \]$ \
  #emph[Effect:] High-activation channels get finer quantization
  (smaller effective $Delta$). \
  #emph[Empirical result:] 64% error reduction on LLMs compared to naive
  quantization. \
  #emph[Cost:] One-time calibration pass (500-1000 samples), no runtime
  overhead.

#block[
#strong[Recommended Values:]

- #strong[Temperature ($tau$):] 3.0 to 5.0 \
  #emph[Why:] Lower ($tau < 2$) makes teacher over-confident, student
  learns only top predictions. Higher ($tau > 6$) makes distribution too
  uniform, loses information. \
  #emph[Compute Impact:] No memory/speed impact, just affects softmax
  before loss. \
  #emph[Tuning:] Start with 3.0; increase if student accuracy plateaus
  early.

- #strong[Lambda ($lambda_(upright("quant"))$):] $5 times 10^(- 4)$ to
  $1 times 10^(- 3)$ \
  #emph[Why:] Must be small because $cal(L)_(upright("quant"))$
  accumulates over millions of weights. Typical ratio:
  $lambda cal(L)_(upright("quant")) \/ cal(L)_(upright("KD")) approx 1 : 1000$.
  \
  #emph[Too high:] Weights collapse to grid, KD loss increases. \
  #emph[Too low:] No quantization awareness, deployment accuracy drops.
  \
  #emph[Diagnostic:] If $cal(L)_(upright("quant"))$ dominates (ratio $>$
  1:100), reduce $lambda$.

- #strong[Bits:] INT8 (conservative), INT4 (aggressive) \
  #emph[INT8:] Typically \<1% accuracy drop, safe for most applications.
  \
  #emph[INT4:] 2-5% accuracy drop, but 8$times$ memory reduction vs
  FP16. \
  #emph[INT3:] Experimental, usually \>5% drop unless extreme QAKD
  tuning. \
  #emph[Memory formula:] Model size = params $times$ bits / 8 bytes. \
  Example: 7B params $times$ 4 bits / 8 = 3.5GB (vs 14GB FP16).

- #strong[Learning Rate:] $2 times 10^(- 5)$ to $5 times 10^(- 5)$ \
  #emph[Why:] Same range as standard fine-tuning; student starts from
  pre-trained weights. \
  #emph[Warmup:] Use 100-500 steps (10% of total) to prevent initial
  instability. \
  #emph[Scheduler:] Cosine decay works well; reduces LR to 10% of
  initial by end.

- #strong[Batch Size:] 16-32 for 24GB GPU, 64-128 for 80GB GPU \
  #emph[Memory breakdown:] Teacher (frozen, inference only) + Student
  (training) + Gradients + Optimizer states (AdamW stores 2$times$
  params). \
  #emph[Gradient accumulation:] Simulate large batch on small GPU:
  `batch_size=8, accum=8 `$arrow.r$` effective=64`. \
  #emph[Trade-off:] Larger batch = smoother gradients, but longer
  per-epoch time.

- #strong[Gradient Scaling (FP16):] Start with $S = 1024$ \
  #emph[Purpose:] Prevent underflow for small gradients
  ($< 6 times 10^(- 8)$ underflows in FP16). \
  #emph[Adaptive:] `GradScaler` auto-adjusts: doubles $S$ if no
  overflow, halves if overflow. \
  #emph[Symptom of wrong $S$:] Loss becomes NaN (overflow) or stops
  decreasing (underflow).

