<fig:llmops_lifecycle_experiment>

#strong[Phase 7: Retraining and Model Updates]

Models are retrained when:

+ #strong[Scheduled Updates:] Regular retraining (e.g., quarterly) with
  fresh data

+ #strong[Performance Degradation:] Accuracy drops below threshold

+ #strong[Data Drift:] Input distribution shifts significantly

+ #strong[New Requirements:] Business needs change (new language
  support, domain expansion)

#strong[Retraining Decision Framework:]

Define a retraining trigger condition:
$ upright("Retrain") = cases(delim: "{", upright("True") & upright("if ") Delta_(upright("accuracy")) < - theta_1 upright(" OR ") D_(upright("KL")) \( upright("prod") \, upright("train") \) > theta_2, upright("False") & upright("otherwise")) $

Where:

- $Delta_(upright("accuracy"))$: Change in accuracy vs baseline (e.g.,
  \-5%)

- $D_(upright("KL"))$: KL divergence between production inputs and
  training distribution

- $theta_1 \, theta_2$: Thresholds tuned to application requirements

=== Model Registry Architecture
<model-registry-architecture>
#strong[Practical Implementation:]

A production model registry provides:

+ #strong[Storage:] Centralized checkpoint storage with versioning

+ #strong[Metadata:] Searchable database of model properties

+ #strong[Access Control:] Role-based permissions (who can deploy?)

+ #strong[Lineage Tracking:] Dependency graph (model A fine-tuned from
  model B)

+ #strong[Deployment Integration:] Seamless handoff to serving
  infrastructure

#strong[Popular Registry Solutions:]

- #strong[MLflow Model Registry:] Open-source, integrates with MLflow
  tracking

- #strong[Hugging Face Hub:] Specialized for transformer models,
  built-in versioning

- #strong[Weights & Biases Artifacts:] Tracks model lineage with
  experiment runs

- #strong[Custom Solutions:] S3 + DynamoDB or GCS + Firestore for
  metadata

== Continuous Training and Evaluation Pipelines
<sec:continuous-evaluation>
=== The Need for Continuous Evaluation
<the-need-for-continuous-evaluation>
#strong[Why Static Evaluation Is Insufficient:]

Traditional ML evaluation assumes #emph[stationary data
distributions];---the test set distribution matches production. This
assumption breaks for LLMs in production:

+ #strong[Temporal Drift:] Language evolves. A model trained in 2022
  doesn't know about \"ChatGPT,\" \"Threads,\" or \"Midjourney.\"

+ #strong[User Behavior Shift:] Users adapt their prompts based on model
  responses. If the model struggles with medical queries, users may
  switch to simpler vocabulary, changing the input distribution.

+ #strong[Adversarial Adaptation:] Bad actors learn to bypass safety
  filters. A jailbreak prompt blocked today spawns 10 variations
  tomorrow.

+ #strong[Domain Expansion:] A customer service chatbot initially
  handles billing questions. Six months later, it must also handle
  technical support, refunds, and product recommendations---new domains
  not in training data.

#strong[Continuous Evaluation Framework:]

Instead of one-time testing, implement ongoing evaluation:

$ upright("Eval")_t = f \( upright("Model")_t \, upright("Data")_t \, upright("Metrics")_t \) $

Run evaluation at regular intervals (daily, weekly) on:

- #strong[Static Benchmarks:] Canonical datasets (MMLU, HellaSwag) for
  tracking regression

- #strong[Dynamic Samples:] Recent production data representing current
  distribution

- #strong[Adversarial Tests:] Newly discovered failure modes

#strong[Evaluation Frequency Trade-Off:]

More frequent evaluation provides faster issue detection but increases
cost. Define evaluation cost:

$ upright("Cost")_(upright("eval")) = upright("Benchmark Size") times upright("Inference Cost per Sample") $

For MMLU (14,000 questions) on GPT-3.5 (\$0.0015/1K tokens, avg 300
tokens/question):
$ upright("Cost")_(upright("eval")) = 14000 times 300 / 1000 times 0.0015 = \$ 6.30 $

Daily evaluation: \$6.30 $times$ 365 = \$2,300/year. Cheap enough for
daily monitoring.

For GPT-4 (\$0.03/1K tokens), same evaluation costs \$126/day =
\$46K/year. Consider weekly evaluation instead.

=== Automated Benchmarking Pipeline
<automated-benchmarking-pipeline>
#strong[Pipeline Architecture:]

A production evaluation pipeline consists of:

$ upright("Pipeline") = { upright("Scheduler") arrow.r upright("Data Loader") arrow.r upright("Inference") arrow.r upright("Metrics") arrow.r upright("Storage") arrow.r upright("Alerting") } $

#strong[Step 1: Scheduler]

Use cron or Airflow to trigger evaluation:

- #strong[Time-based:] Daily at 2 AM UTC (low traffic period)

- #strong[Event-based:] After model deployment, after significant
  traffic changes

- #strong[Threshold-based:] When production error rate exceeds baseline

#strong[Step 2: Data Loader]

Load benchmark data with proper versioning. Critical requirement:
#emph[benchmark data must remain constant] across evaluation runs to
enable trend analysis. If you change the test set, accuracy changes
become meaningless.

#strong[Step 3: Inference Engine]

Run model inference with consistent settings:

- #strong[Temperature:] 0.0 (deterministic) for reproducibility

- #strong[Max tokens:] Fixed limit based on benchmark requirements

- #strong[Batching:] Group requests for efficiency (batch size 16-32)

#strong[Step 4: Metrics Computation]

Calculate benchmark-specific metrics. For MMLU:

$ upright("Accuracy") = upright("Correct Answers") / upright("Total Questions") $

For open-ended generation (e.g., summarization):

$ upright("ROUGE-L") = frac(2 times upright("Precision") times upright("Recall"), upright("Precision") + upright("Recall")) $

#strong[Step 5: Result Storage]

Store results in time-series database for trend analysis:

$ upright("Result")_t = \( upright("timestamp") \, upright("model_version") \, upright("metrics") \, upright("metadata") \) $

Recommended databases:

- #strong[InfluxDB:] Purpose-built for time-series data, excellent for
  metrics

- #strong[Prometheus:] Standard for monitoring, integrates with Grafana

- #strong[PostgreSQL + TimescaleDB:] Relational database with
  time-series extension

#strong[Step 6: Alerting]

Define alert rules based on business requirements:

$ upright("Alert") = cases(delim: "{", upright("True") & upright("if ") upright("Accuracy")_t < upright("Threshold")_(upright("min")), upright("True") & upright("if ") \( upright("Accuracy")_(t - 1) - upright("Accuracy")_t \) > Delta_(upright("max")), upright("False") & upright("otherwise")) $

Example thresholds:

- #strong[Absolute:] Accuracy \< 65% (model broken)

- #strong[Relative:] Drop \> 5% from previous week (performance
  degradation)

- #strong[Trend:] Declining accuracy over 3+ consecutive evaluations
  (systematic drift)

