#strong[What to do when alert fires:]

+ Check recent deployments (new model version? config change?)

+ Inspect failed examples (what types of questions are failing now?)

+ Check data drift metrics (input distribution changed?)

+ Review infrastructure logs (GPU memory issues? timeout errors?)

#strong[Key Takeaways - Production Lessons Learned:]

+ #strong[Subsampling works:] 1K samples $approx$ full benchmark for
  trend monitoring, 10x faster

+ #strong[Determinism is non-negotiable:] Temperature=0.0 or metrics
  become meaningless

+ #strong[Alerting thresholds matter:] 5% threshold balances false
  positives vs detection speed

+ #strong[Storage enables debugging:] Storing predictions (not just
  accuracy) lets you inspect failures

+ #strong[Automation is essential:] Daily evaluation catches issues
  before users complain

#strong[\2. Subsampling strategy:]

Daily evaluation uses 100 samples for speed: 100 samples $times$ 0.3
sec/sample = 30 seconds total. Weekly evaluation uses full dataset:
1,531 samples $times$ 0.3 sec/sample = 7.6 minutes.

This balances cost (GPU time) vs sensitivity (detecting smaller accuracy
changes).

#strong[\3. Reproducible subsampling:]

`random.seed(42)` ensures the same 100 samples are selected each day.
This is critical: if you randomly sample different subsets daily,
accuracy will fluctuate due to sampling variance, not real performance
changes.

#strong[\4. Alert threshold tuning:]

5% threshold is empirically validated: smaller drops (1-2%) are often
noise; larger drops (\>5%) almost always indicate real issues. Adjust
based on your model's typical variance.

== Dataset Drift and Model Degradation Analysis
<sec:drift-detection>
=== Understanding Data Drift
<understanding-data-drift>
#strong[The Problem:]

Your model was trained on data from 2022. It's now 2024. Users are
asking about events, products, and concepts that didn't exist in
training data. Even if the model's weights haven't changed, its
#emph[effective performance] degrades because the input distribution has
shifted.

#strong[Formal Definition of Distribution Drift:]

Let $P_(upright("train")) \( x \)$ be the training data distribution and
$P_(upright("prod")) \( x \)$ be the production data distribution at
time $t$. Drift occurs when:

$ D \( P_(upright("train")) \, P_(upright("prod")) \) > theta $

Where $D \( dot.op \, dot.op \)$ is a distribution distance metric (KL
divergence, Wasserstein distance, etc.) and $theta$ is an
application-specific threshold.

#strong[Types of Drift:]

+ #strong[Covariate Drift:] Input distribution changes, but relationship
  $P \( y \| x \)$ remains stable.

  Example: E-commerce chatbot trained on desktop users (long queries)
  now serves mobile users (short queries). The intent $y$ given query
  $x$ is the same, but query length distribution shifted.

  $ P_(upright("train")) \( x \) eq.not P_(upright("prod")) \( x \) \, quad upright("but") quad P_(upright("train")) \( y \| x \) = P_(upright("prod")) \( y \| x \) $

+ #strong[Concept Drift:] Relationship $P \( y \| x \)$ changes over
  time.

  Example: Sentiment analysis model trained pre-COVID. The phrase \"stay
  home\" was negative (boredom, isolation). Post-COVID, same phrase can
  be positive (safety, responsibility). Same input, different meaning.

  $ P_(upright("train")) \( y \| x \) eq.not P_(upright("prod")) \( y \| x \) $

+ #strong[Label Drift:] Output distribution changes (rare for LLMs).

  Example: Content moderation system where user behavior shifts---fewer
  hate speech reports in production than training.

  $ P_(upright("train")) \( y \) eq.not P_(upright("prod")) \( y \) $

=== Measuring Distribution Drift
<measuring-distribution-drift>
#strong[Method 1: KL Divergence]

Kullback-Leibler divergence measures how one distribution differs from
another:

$ D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) = sum_x P \( x \) log frac(P \( x \), Q \( x \)) $

For continuous distributions (e.g., embedding spaces):

$ D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) = integral P \( x \) log frac(P \( x \), Q \( x \)) d x $

#strong[Properties:]

- $D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) gt.eq 0$, with equality iff
  $P = Q$

- #emph[Not symmetric:]
  $D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) eq.not D_(upright("KL")) \( Q parallel P \)$

- Undefined if $Q \( x \) = 0$ but $P \( x \) > 0$ (use smoothing)

#strong[Practical Application:]

Compute KL divergence between training and production token
distributions:

$ P_(upright("train")) \( upright("token") \) & = frac(upright("Count")_(upright("train")) \( upright("token") \), sum_(upright("all tokens")) upright("Count")_(upright("train")))\
P_(upright("prod")) \( upright("token") \) & = frac(upright("Count")_(upright("prod")) \( upright("token") \), sum_(upright("all tokens")) upright("Count")_(upright("prod"))) $

Then:
$ D_(upright("KL")) = sum_(upright("tokens")) P_(upright("train")) \( upright("token") \) log frac(P_(upright("train")) \( upright("token") \), P_(upright("prod")) \( upright("token") \)) $

#strong[Interpretation:]

- $D_(upright("KL")) < 0.01$: Negligible drift

- $0.01 lt.eq D_(upright("KL")) < 0.1$: Moderate drift, monitor closely

- $D_(upright("KL")) gt.eq 0.1$: Significant drift, consider retraining

#strong[Method 2: Population Stability Index (PSI)]

PSI is a standard metric in industry for detecting distribution shifts:

$ upright("PSI") = sum_(i = 1)^(n_(upright("bins"))) (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) - P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) times ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) $<eq:psi_foundation>

Where distributions are binned into $n_(upright("bins"))$ buckets
(typically 10-20 bins), $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \))$ is the
proportion of production data in bin $i$, and
$P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))$ is the proportion of baseline
(training) data in bin $i$.

#strong[Mathematical Derivation of PSI:]

#emph[Step 1: Motivation from Information Theory]

PSI is derived from the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, which measures
the difference between two probability distributions. The KL divergence
from distribution $Q$ to distribution $P$ is:

$ D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) = sum_(i = 1)^n P \( i \) ln (frac(P \( i \), Q \( i \))) $

#emph[Intuition:] KL divergence quantifies how many extra bits are
needed to encode data from distribution $P$ using a code optimized for
distribution $Q$. In our context, $P$ is the production distribution and
$Q$ is the baseline (expected) distribution.

#emph[Step 2: Symmetrization for Practical Use]

KL divergence is asymmetric:
$D_(upright("KL")) \( P parallel Q \) eq.not D_(upright("KL")) \( Q parallel P \)$.
For drift detection, we want a symmetric measure that treats both
distributions equally. PSI modifies KL divergence by:

$ upright("PSI") & = sum_(i = 1)^n (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) - P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) times ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)))\
 & = sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) - sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) $

#emph[Step 3: Expanding the Formula]

Let's expand the second term using logarithm properties:

$ sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) & = sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) [ln \( P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) \) - ln \( P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) \)] $

Substituting back into the PSI formula:

$ upright("PSI") & = sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) - sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) ln \( P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) \) + sum_(i = 1)^n P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) ln \( P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) \) $

#emph[Step 4: Interpretation of Each Term]

- #strong[Term 1:]
  $sum_i P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)))$
  \= Forward KL divergence
  $D_(upright("KL")) \( P_(upright("prod")) parallel P_(upright("baseline")) \)$

- #strong[Term 2 - Term 3:] Cross-entropy adjustment ensuring symmetry

The key insight: PSI is a #emph[weighted sum of logarithmic ratios];,
where the weight is the #emph[difference in proportions] between
production and baseline.

#emph[Step 5: Why Logarithmic Ratios?]

The logarithm serves two purposes:

+ #strong[Scale invariance:] $ln (0.20 / 0.10) = ln \( 2 \) = 0.693$ has
  the same magnitude as $ln (0.10 / 0.05) = ln \( 2 \)$. Doubling of
  probability is treated equally regardless of absolute values.

+ #strong[Sensitivity to small probabilities:] When
  $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) arrow.r 0$ but
  $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \)) > 0$ (or vice versa),
  $ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))) arrow.r - oo$,
  strongly penalizing disappeared bins.

#strong[Concrete Example: Sentiment Distribution Drift]

Suppose we deployed a sentiment classifier trained on balanced data.
After 3 months in production, we observe a shift in input sentiment
distribution.

#emph[Baseline Distribution (Training Data):]

- Bin 1 (Very Negative): $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 1 \)) = 0.20$

- Bin 2 (Negative): $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 2 \)) = 0.30$

- Bin 3 (Neutral): $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 3 \)) = 0.30$

- Bin 4 (Positive): $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 4 \)) = 0.15$

- Bin 5 (Very Positive): $P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 5 \)) = 0.05$

#emph[Production Distribution (Current Data):]

- Bin 1 (Very Negative): $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 1 \)) = 0.10$ (halved)

- Bin 2 (Negative): $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 2 \)) = 0.25$ (decreased)

- Bin 3 (Neutral): $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 3 \)) = 0.35$ (increased)

- Bin 4 (Positive): $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 4 \)) = 0.20$ (increased)

- Bin 5 (Very Positive): $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 5 \)) = 0.10$
  (doubled)

#emph[Step-by-Step PSI Calculation:]

#strong[Bin 1:]
$ upright("Contribution")_1 & = \( P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 1 \)) - P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 1 \)) \) times ln (P_(upright("prod"))^(\( 1 \)) / P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( 1 \)))\
 & = \( 0.10 - 0.20 \) times ln (0.10 / 0.20)\
 & = \( - 0.10 \) times ln \( 0.5 \)\
 & = \( - 0.10 \) times \( - 0.693 \)\
 & = 0.0693 $

#strong[Bin 2:]
$ upright("Contribution")_2 & = \( 0.25 - 0.30 \) times ln (0.25 / 0.30)\
 & = \( - 0.05 \) times ln \( 0.833 \)\
 & = \( - 0.05 \) times \( - 0.182 \)\
 & = 0.0091 $

#strong[Bin 3:]
$ upright("Contribution")_3 & = \( 0.35 - 0.30 \) times ln (0.35 / 0.30)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times ln \( 1.167 \)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times \( 0.154 \)\
 & = 0.0077 $

#strong[Bin 4:]
$ upright("Contribution")_4 & = \( 0.20 - 0.15 \) times ln (0.20 / 0.15)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times ln \( 1.333 \)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times \( 0.287 \)\
 & = 0.0144 $

#strong[Bin 5:]
$ upright("Contribution")_5 & = \( 0.10 - 0.05 \) times ln (0.10 / 0.05)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times ln \( 2.0 \)\
 & = \( 0.05 \) times \( 0.693 \)\
 & = 0.0347 $

#emph[Total PSI:]
$ upright("PSI") & = 0.0693 + 0.0091 + 0.0077 + 0.0144 + 0.0347\
 & = 0.1352 $

#strong[Interpretation:] PSI = 0.1352 falls in the #strong[moderate
drift] range (0.1 $lt.eq$ PSI \< 0.2), indicating the sentiment
distribution has shifted enough to warrant investigation but not severe
enough to immediately retrain.

#strong[Key Observations from Example:]

+ #strong[Sign of contribution:]

  - When
    $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) < P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))$
    (bin decreased): difference is negative, log ratio is negative,
    product is #strong[positive]

  - When
    $P_(upright("prod"))^(\( i \)) > P_(upright("baseline"))^(\( i \))$
    (bin increased): difference is positive, log ratio is positive,
    product is #strong[positive]

  - PSI is #strong[always non-negative] (all contributions add
    positively)

+ #strong[Magnitude sensitivity:]

  - Bin 1 (halved from 0.20 to 0.10) contributes 0.0693 (largest)

  - Bin 5 (doubled from 0.05 to 0.10) contributes 0.0347 (second
    largest)

  - Small absolute changes in large bins matter more than large relative
    changes in small bins

+ #strong[Balanced sensitivity:] PSI detects both increases and
  decreases equally, making it suitable for two-sided drift detection

#strong[Why This Formula Works for Drift Detection:]

- #strong[Mathematical foundation:] Rooted in information theory (KL
  divergence)

- #strong[Symmetry:] Treats production and baseline distributions
  equally

- #strong[Additivity:] Total drift is sum of per-bin drifts

- #strong[Interpretable thresholds:] Industry-validated ranges (\< 0.1,
  0.1-0.2, \> 0.2)

- #strong[Computational efficiency:] $O \( n_(upright("bins")) \)$
  complexity, typically $n_(upright("bins")) approx 10$

#strong[PSI Interpretation:]

- PSI \< 0.1: No significant drift

- 0.1 $lt.eq$ PSI \< 0.2: Moderate drift, investigate

- PSI $gt.eq$ 0.2: Severe drift, model likely degraded

#strong[Method 3: Embedding Space Analysis]

For LLMs, a powerful approach is comparing embedding distributions:

+ Encode training samples:
  ${ e_1^(upright("train")) \, e_2^(upright("train")) \, dots.h \, e_n^(upright("train")) }$

+ Encode production samples:
  ${ e_1^(upright("prod")) \, e_2^(upright("prod")) \, dots.h \, e_m^(upright("prod")) }$

+ Compute Wasserstein distance (Earth Mover's Distance):

$ W_p \( P \, Q \) = (inf_(gamma in Gamma \( P \, Q \)) integral parallel x - y parallel^p d gamma \( x \, y \))^(1 \/ p) $

For $p = 2$ (Wasserstein-2 distance), this measures the minimum \"cost\"
to transform distribution $P$ into $Q$.

#strong[Practical Implementation:] Use
`scipy.stats.wasserstein_distance` on 2D PCA projections of embeddings
for visualization and quantification.

