== Monitoring Metrics: Latency, Cost, and Token Efficiency
<sec:llmops-monitoring>
=== Latency Monitoring
<latency-monitoring>
#strong[Key Latency Metrics:]

+ #strong[Time to First Token (TTFT):]

  Time from request to first output token. Critical for perceived
  responsiveness.

  $ upright("TTFT") = t_(upright("first_token")) - t_(upright("request")) $

  Target: TTFT \< 200ms for interactive applications.

+ #strong[Time Per Output Token (TPOT):]

  Average time to generate each subsequent token.

  $ upright("TPOT") = frac(t_(upright("last_token")) - t_(upright("first_token")), n_(upright("tokens")) - 1) $

  Target: TPOT \< 50ms for smooth streaming.

+ #strong[End-to-End Latency:]

  Total time from request to complete response.

  $ upright("Latency")_(upright("e2e")) = upright("TTFT") + \( n_(upright("tokens")) - 1 \) times upright("TPOT") $

#strong[Example Calculation:]

For a 100-token response with TTFT = 150ms and TPOT = 30ms:

$ upright("Latency")_(upright("e2e")) = 150 + \( 100 - 1 \) times 30 = 150 + 2970 = 3120 upright(" ms") = 3.12 upright(" seconds") $

#strong[Percentile Tracking:]

Don't just track average latency---monitor p50, p95, p99:

- #strong[p50 (median):] Typical user experience

- #strong[p95:] 95% of users see this or better

- #strong[p99:] Worst-case for most users (outliers)

Alert if p99 exceeds SLA threshold (e.g., p99 \> 5 seconds).

=== Cost Monitoring and Optimization
<cost-monitoring-and-optimization>
#strong[Cost Equation for LLM Serving:]

$ C_(upright("total")) = C_(upright("compute")) + C_(upright("storage")) + C_(upright("network")) + C_(upright("overhead")) $

#strong[Compute Cost:]

The dominant cost is GPU inference:

$ C_(upright("compute")) = frac(upright("Requests") times upright("Avg Tokens") times upright("Time per Token") times upright("GPU Cost/Hour"), 3600) $

For 10M requests/month, 200 tokens avg, 30ms per token, \$3/hour GPU:

$ C_(upright("compute")) & = frac(10 times 10^6 times 200 times 0.03 times 3, 3600)\
 & = frac(180 times 10^6, 3600)\
 & = \$ 50 \, 000 \/ upright("month") $

#strong[Cost Optimization Strategies:]

+ #strong[Caching:] Cache responses for repeated queries

  $ upright("Savings")_(upright("cache")) = upright("Hit Rate") times C_(upright("compute")) $

  With 30% cache hit rate: \$50K $times$ 0.3 = \$15K/month savings

+ #strong[Batching:] Process multiple requests together

  Batch size 16 vs individual requests: 5x throughput → 5x fewer GPUs
  needed

+ #strong[Model Quantization:] Use INT8/INT4 instead of FP16

  2-4x speedup → 50-75% cost reduction

+ #strong[Prompt Compression:] Reduce input token count

  If avg prompt is 500 tokens but can be compressed to 300 tokens: 40%
  cost reduction on input processing

=== Token Efficiency Metrics
<token-efficiency-metrics>
#strong[Tokens per Dollar:]

For cost comparison across providers:

$ upright("Token Efficiency") = upright("Total Tokens Processed") / upright("Total Cost ($)") $

Example comparison:

- GPT-4: \$0.03/1K tokens → 33,333 tokens/\$

- GPT-3.5: \$0.002/1K tokens → 500,000 tokens/\$

- Self-hosted GPT-2: \$0.0001/1K tokens → 10,000,000 tokens/\$

#strong[Effective Token Usage:]

Not all tokens are equally valuable. Define token utility:

$ upright("Token Utility") = frac(upright("Output Tokens"), upright("Input Tokens") + upright("Output Tokens")) $

High utility: User asks short question (10 tokens), gets detailed answer
(200 tokens). Utility = 200/210 = 95%.

Low utility: User provides long context (2000 tokens), gets short answer
(20 tokens). Utility = 20/2020 = 1%.

Optimize prompts to maximize token utility---reduce unnecessary context.

