#block[
+ #strong[LLMOps is Continuous, Not One-Time:] Unlike traditional
  software deployment, LLMs require ongoing monitoring, evaluation, and
  retraining. Plan for 6-12 month retraining cycles minimum.

+ #strong[Core Monitoring Equation:]
  $ upright("System Health") = f \( upright("Accuracy") \, upright("Latency") \, upright("Cost") \, upright("Drift") \) $

  #strong[Derivation of System Health Metric:]

  #emph[Step 1: Define normalized component scores.] Each metric is
  normalized to \[0, 1\] range where 1 = perfect, 0 = unacceptable:
  $ S_(upright("acc")) & = frac(upright("Current Accuracy") - upright("Minimum Acceptable"), upright("Target Accuracy") - upright("Minimum Acceptable"))\
  S_(upright("lat")) & = frac(upright("SLA Threshold") - upright("Current p99 Latency"), upright("SLA Threshold") - upright("Best Observed"))\
  S_(upright("cost")) & = frac(upright("Budget") - upright("Current Cost"), upright("Budget"))\
  S_(upright("drift")) & = 1 - upright("Current Drift Score") / upright("Critical Drift Threshold") $

  #emph[Step 2: Apply weighted aggregation.] Different applications
  prioritize different metrics. A weighted sum captures business
  priorities:
  $ upright("System Health") = w_1 S_(upright("acc")) + w_2 S_(upright("lat")) + w_3 S_(upright("cost")) + w_4 S_(upright("drift")) $

  where $w_1 + w_2 + w_3 + w_4 = 1$ and weights reflect importance.
  Example weights:

  - Medical chatbot: $\( 0.5 \, 0.2 \, 0.1 \, 0.2 \)$ - accuracy
    critical

  - E-commerce recommendations: $\( 0.3 \, 0.3 \, 0.3 \, 0.1 \)$ -
    balanced

  - Internal tool: $\( 0.2 \, 0.1 \, 0.5 \, 0.2 \)$ - cost-sensitive

  #emph[Step 3: Define alert thresholds.] Trigger alerts when health
  drops below thresholds:
  $ upright("Critical Alert") & upright(" if ") upright("System Health") < 0.5\
  upright("Warning Alert") & upright(" if ") upright("System Health") < 0.7\
  upright("Healthy") & upright(" if ") upright("System Health") gt.eq 0.7 $

  #strong[Why this formulation?] The weighted sum ensures no single
  metric can be gamed. For instance, achieving 100% accuracy
  ($S_(upright("acc")) = 1$) but with 10$times$ over-budget costs
  ($S_(upright("cost")) = - 9$) yields negative health score, forcing
  corrective action.

  #strong[Practical Example:] Medical diagnosis system with targets: 85%
  accuracy (min 70%), p99 \< 500ms (current 450ms), \$10K/month budget
  (current \$8K), drift \< 0.2 (current 0.1):
  $ S_(upright("acc")) & = frac(0.85 - 0.70, 0.90 - 0.70) = 0.75\
  S_(upright("lat")) & = frac(500 - 450, 500 - 200) = 0.167\
  S_(upright("cost")) & = frac(10000 - 8000, 10000) = 0.2\
  S_(upright("drift")) & = 1 - 0.1 / 0.2 = 0.5\
  upright("System Health") & = 0.5 \( 0.75 \) + 0.2 \( 0.167 \) + 0.1 \( 0.2 \) + 0.2 \( 0.5 \)\
   & = 0.375 + 0.033 + 0.02 + 0.1 = 0.528 upright(" (Critical Alert!)") $

  Track all four continuously. Optimizing one at expense of others leads
  to production issues.

+ #strong[Drift Detection Hierarchy:]

  - #strong[Fastest:] Token distribution (KL divergence)---compute in
    minutes

  - #strong[Most Accurate:] Embedding space (Wasserstein)---captures
    semantic shifts

  - #strong[Most Interpretable:] PSI---industry standard with clear
    thresholds

  Use all three for comprehensive monitoring.

+ #strong[Retraining Decision Framework:]
  $ upright("Retrain if: ") \( Delta_(upright("acc")) > 5 % \) upright(" OR ") \( D_(upright("KL")) > 0.1 upright(" AND trend worsening") \) $

  #strong[Derivation of Retraining Decision Logic:]

  #emph[Step 1: Define accuracy degradation metric.] Measure relative
  drop from deployment baseline:
  $ Delta_(upright("acc")) = frac(upright("Accuracy")_(upright("baseline")) - upright("Accuracy")_(upright("current")), upright("Accuracy")_(upright("baseline"))) times 100 % $

  Example: Deployed at 85% accuracy, now at 80%:
  $Delta_(upright("acc")) = frac(85 - 80, 85) times 100 % = 5.88 %$

  #emph[Step 2: Define distribution drift metric.] KL divergence
  measures how production data distribution diverges from training
  distribution:
  $ D_(upright("KL")) \( P_(upright("prod")) parallel P_(upright("train")) \) = sum_x P_(upright("prod")) \( x \) log frac(P_(upright("prod")) \( x \), P_(upright("train")) \( x \)) $

  where $P_(upright("prod"))$ is current production token distribution
  and $P_(upright("train"))$ is original training distribution. Higher
  $D_(upright("KL"))$ means data has shifted significantly.

  #emph[Step 3: Define trend direction.] Track whether drift is
  accelerating:
  $ upright("Trend Worsening") = cases(delim: "{", upright("True") & upright("if ") frac(d D_(upright("KL")), d t) > 0 upright(" for past 4 weeks"), upright("False") & upright("otherwise")) $

  #emph[Step 4: Establish statistical thresholds.] Why 5% accuracy drop
  and 0.1 KL divergence?

  #strong[Accuracy threshold justification:] Statistical significance
  and business impact:

  - 5% drop is typically #strong[2-3 standard deviations] from normal
    variation on validation set

  - Example: Model with 85$plus.minus$1% validation accuracy has std dev
    $sigma approx 1.7 %$. A 5% drop = $5 % \/ 1.7 % = 2.94 sigma$ (99.8%
    confidence it's real degradation, not noise)

  - Business impact: 85% $arrow.r$ 80% accuracy can double error rate
    (15% $arrow.r$ 20% errors = 1.33$times$ increase in customer
    complaints)

  #strong[KL divergence threshold justification:] Empirical calibration
  from industry:

  - $D_(upright("KL")) < 0.05$: Normal seasonal variation (holidays,
    weekends)

  - $D_(upright("KL")) = 0.05$-$0.1$: Noticeable drift, monitor closely

  - $D_(upright("KL")) > 0.1$: Significant distribution shift requiring
    action

  - Example: $D_(upright("KL")) = 0.1$ means production data has
    $e^0.1 approx 1.105 times$ higher entropy relative to training data
    \- model is encountering 10% more \"surprise\"

  #emph[Step 5: Combine with OR logic.] Use disjunction (OR) to catch
  two failure modes:
  $ upright("Retrain") = underbrace(\( Delta_(upright("acc")) > 5 % \), upright("Sudden Performance Drop")) #h(0em) upright("OR") #h(0em) underbrace(\( D_(upright("KL")) > 0.1 upright(" AND trend worsening") \), upright("Gradual Distribution Shift")) $

  #strong[Why OR not AND?] Must catch both failure modes:

  - #strong[Sudden drop scenario:] Model breaks immediately (e.g., new
    competitor product launches, model sees new attack vectors).
    Accuracy drops overnight even if $D_(upright("KL"))$ hasn't reached
    0.1 yet. Must retrain immediately.

  - #strong[Gradual drift scenario:] Accuracy drops slowly (84%
    $arrow.r$ 83% $arrow.r$ 82%) over months due to changing user
    behavior. Each individual drop $< 5 %$ so doesn't trigger alone, but
    $D_(upright("KL"))$ steadily increases. AND operator ensures we
    don't retrain on noisy KL spikes.

  #strong[Practical Example:] E-commerce recommendation system:

  - #strong[Month 1:] Accuracy = 85%, $D_(upright("KL")) = 0.03$
    $arrow.r$ No retrain (healthy)

  - #strong[Month 3:] Accuracy = 84%, $D_(upright("KL")) = 0.08$
    $arrow.r$ No retrain ($Delta_(upright("acc")) = 1.2 %$ $< 5 %$,
    drift below 0.1)

  - #strong[Month 5:] Accuracy = 83%, $D_(upright("KL")) = 0.12$, trend
    worsening $arrow.r$ #strong[RETRAIN!] (KL condition met)

  - #strong[Alternative scenario (sudden drop):] New product category
    added, accuracy drops to 79% overnight $arrow.r$ #strong[RETRAIN!]
    ($Delta_(upright("acc")) = 7 %$ $> 5 %$)

  Don't retrain on single bad week---wait for sustained degradation
  signal.

